  <\quotation>
    An analysis written by an AI model (GPT-6 Astra), from the Lean source
    and the literature listed at the end. It asks which parts of the proof
    follow Balaban's programme, which follow OpenAI's 2026 sigma-model
    papers, which are standard and which appear new. It identifies the
    closest antecedents among the sources read, not borrowing or priority. It
    has not been reviewed by the authors of the works it discusses.
  </quotation>

  <strong|Contents>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><hlink|Main conclusions|#1-main-conclusions>

    <item><hlink|The source toolkits|#2-the-source-toolkits>

    <item><hlink|Component-by-component provenance|#3-component-by-component-provenance>

    <item><hlink|Cross-cutting comparisons|#4-cross-cutting-comparisons>

    <item><hlink|What appears new, and what is
    routine|#5-what-appears-new-and-what-is-routine>

    <item><hlink|Open questions and limits|#6-open-questions-limits-and-priorities-for-expert-checking>

    <item><hlink|Bibliography and source access|#7-bibliography-and-source-access>

    <item><hlink|Source keys|#source-keys>
  </itemize-minus>

  Short keys such as B-RG-I (Baªaban's papers), N2 (OpenAI's O(4) paper) or
  GK85 are listed in the <hlink|source keys|#source-keys> at the end; full
  publication details and reading limits are in
  <hlink|Ÿ7|#7-bibliography-and-source-access>.

  <section|1. Main conclusions><label|1-main-conclusions>

  <strong|N2 remains the closest inspected match for the particular
  ultraviolet density representation; the gauge analytic core has stronger
  direct antecedents in Baªaban, and several apparently distinctive polymer
  devices are already in Gaw¦dzki\UKupiainen.> The match to <em|Sharp mass
  bounds for the two-dimensional O(4) model> (N2), with developments in the
  canonical O(3) paper (N3), concerns a conjunction of formulas. It does not
  establish that the proof's ideas as a whole are closer to the NLSM papers.
  The five 1985 gauge papers directly supply nonlinear averages, controlled
  changes of gauge, analytic stationary backgrounds and regular background
  propagators. Magnen\URivasseau\USénéor supplies a further substantial
  four-dimensional gauge UV construction. Neither those antecedents nor the
  NLSM comparison supplies the exact stated all-composite, phase, stress and
  OPE endpoint. The converse description, \Pessentially Balaban, with the
  remainder just formalization,\Q is also unsupported. Confidence is high in
  the individual formula matches and older antecedents, medium in ranking the
  closest complete representation, and low in claims of worldwide novelty.

  Four observations determine this verdict.

  <\enumerate>
    <item><strong|The exact density format is unusually close to N2.> The
    clipped kinetic term, masked square, regular errors with subtraction
    conditions, compulsory covering inventories, fixed reference thresholds,
    window inverse columns and exact reset occur together in N2 Ÿ5,
    pp.38\U55. The corresponding objects occur in
    <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/PathActions.lean:210\U242>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/PathActions.lean#L210-L242>,
    <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/Activities.lean:42\U129>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/Activities.lean#L42-L129>,
    <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/History.lean:63\U85>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/History.lean#L63-L85>,
    <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Windows.lean:65\U74>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Windows.lean#L65-L74>
    and <hlink|<verbatim|YM/AnalyticBridge/VERIFIED_RESET_STEP/Evaluation.lean:71\U95>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/AnalyticBridge/VERIFIED_RESET_STEP/Evaluation.lean#L71-L95>.
    The conjunction is a close match, substantially adapted to gauge
    variables. Mandatory covering and selective exponentiation alone are
    older: GK85 p.212 Eq.(4.8) and pp.218\U221 Eqs.(5.28)\U(5.53) already
    separate bad-region polymers from the regular exponential.

    <item><strong|Several tempting NLSM attributions are actually older.>
    Balaban already has symmetric nonlinear group averages and normalized
    gauge kernels, B-RG-I pp.252\U254, Eqs.(0.4)\U(0.15), as well as marginal
    Maxwell extraction, pp.258,292\U297. The relevant Lean counterparts are
    <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/Patch.lean:102\U179>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/Patch.lean#L102-L179>
    and <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/Activities.lean:70\U75>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/Activities.lean#L70-L75>.
    B-Gauge Theorem 2 p.83 and B-Var Theorem 1 p.279, Proposition 6
    pp.295\U296 directly establish controlled gauge changes and analytic
    stationary backgrounds. B-Back Theorems 3.1\U3.4 pp.397\U400 gives
    regular-background propagators. Compare
    <hlink|<verbatim|YM/PCalc/Chart.lean:78\U100>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/PCalc/Chart.lean#L78-L100>
    and <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Work/F04.lean:62\U86>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/F04.lean#L62-L86>.
    GK85 p.222 and BY90 pp.360\U363 also make the older order of constants
    and analytic RG control explicit.

    <item><strong|The gauge comparison and continuum identification require
    separate treatment.> The determinant budget at
    <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/RANK/Determinant.lean:240\U274>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/RANK/Determinant.lean#L240-L274>
    cancels gauge-dependent volume powers; it is not N3 Ÿ7's rough-region
    filling argument. The Ward/Maxwell/phase/trace route in
    <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Route.lean:114\U123>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Route.lean#L114-L123>,
    <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Phase/Mixture.lean:65\U86>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Phase/Mixture.lean#L65-L86>
    and <hlink|<verbatim|YM/TraceExtension/Verse/Cape.lean:53\U88>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/Verse/Cape.lean#L53-L88>
    differs from N3 ŸŸ10\U11,15. MRS93 ŸVI pp.368\U369 already controls
    background-dependent gauge/ghost determinant normalization; the exact
    torus cancellation remains unmatched in the passages read, not
    established as novel. Much of reconstruction is standard under the
    corrected OS75 hypotheses; the potential contributions are narrower
    combinations and uniform estimates.

    <item><strong|The endpoints differ on both sides.> B-Large-II, Theorem 1,
    p.355, expressly assumes small effective couplings and leaves physical
    observables for further work, p.356. MRS93 p.327 states a fixed-IR
    ultraviolet Schwinger-function limit and Slavnov identities, while
    expressly leaving a fully detailed proof unwritten; p.329 excludes the
    construction there of renormalized physical composite observables. N3
    Theorems 1.1\U1.2, pp.5\U7, assert a canonical massive limit.
    <hlink|<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean:177\U188>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean#L177-L188>
    instead permits a volume subsequence and does <strong|not> assert a
    positive mass gap. Unique vacuum in each phase is not a mass-gap
    statement: compare <hlink|<verbatim|PublicTheory.lean:270\U278>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/PublicTheory.lean#L270-L278>
    with <hlink|<verbatim|YM/EveryPhase/Decay.lean:173\U192>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/EveryPhase/Decay.lean#L173-L192>.
  </enumerate>

  A defensible size comparison is possible, but <strong|not a measurement of
  intellectual ownership>. The size comparison counts the union of source
  spans of declarations used by the main theorem within each of the 32 units.
  The total within the units is <strong|410,299 lines>; the global union,
  including declarations outside those units, is <strong|410,820>. A
  declaration's span includes statements, proof code and internal comments,
  so this is an implementation weight. It is neither the physical repository
  line count nor a count of mathematical ideas.

