  These are disjoint <strong|groups of units>, not exclusive provenance
  shares. The first group contains considerable Balaban and standard
  mathematics; the second contains standard Gaussian and perturbative
  mathematics. Neither the mixed gauge-comparison group nor the continuum
  group can be attributed wholesale to either author group. The unit
  membership is recorded in Ÿ1.2, and the evidence for each comparison
  follows in Ÿ3. Under a stricter definition, retaining only
  <hlink|III.4|outline/units/III-4.tm>, <hlink|III.8|outline/units/III-8.tm>,
  <hlink|III.9|outline/units/III-9.tm> and
  <hlink|III.10|outline/units/III-10.tm> as NLSM-nearest gives <strong|24.1%>
  of the implementation. Thus the conclusion survives excluding the more
  debatable block-observation, schedule and iteration comparisons. It does
  not justify saying that a majority of all mathematical ideas originated in
  the NLSM papers.

  These conclusions concern <strong|closest mathematical antecedents among
  the sources read>. They do not establish causal borrowing, author
  intentions, access histories, or priority over literature not read here.
  The September\UOctober preprint dates precede the October Lean source
  version, but that chronology alone proves no dependence.

  <subsection|1.1 Scope and vocabulary><label|11-scope-and-vocabulary>

  Claims about the proof's mechanisms rest on the Lean definitions and proofs
  cited below. The <hlink|statement.md|statement.tm>,
  <hlink|summary.md|summary.tm> and unit outlines provide orientation; their
  assertions about the literature are not evidence for these comparisons. The
  counts concern declarations used by the main theorem, as identified by the
  existing dependency information. This is a comparison of mathematical
  mechanisms, not a certification of the Lean theorem or of the six
  preprints.

  \PSame idea/device\Q means a fairly specific construction with the same
  role and structural constraints. \PSame general method\Q means an
  established technique compatible with many implementations. \PSuperficial
  resemblance\Q means common terminology or a common goal without a matched
  mechanism. Those distinctions matter particularly for the words
  <em|sector>, <em|Gaussian>, <em|annulus>, <em|locality>, <em|nonfree> and
  <em|continuum>.

  The classifications used below are:

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><strong|STANDARD:> widely established mathematics or textbook
    physics, including routine functional analysis.

    <item><strong|ADAPTED-BALABAN:> a specific gauge/RG mechanism closely
    matching the inspected Balaban programme.

    <item><strong|ADAPTED-NLSM:> a specific mechanism most closely matching
    one of N1\UN6 in the sources read.

    <item><strong|ADAPTED-OTHER:> an identified older method outside those
    two groups.

    <item><strong|candidate-NEW:> a specific combination, estimate or
    extension for which no matching construction was found; this is a bounded
    novelty claim, not established priority across the literature.
  </itemize-minus>

  A mixture's proportions describe rough mechanism weight within that
  component, rounded to broad increments. They are judgement calls, not
  fractions of proved originality; changing them by 15\U20 percentage points
  is often reasonable. For a split component, the reported size is its
  <strong|parent unit's> allocation unless an actual file size is stated.
  Repeated parent sizes must not be added. Confidence refers to the
  correspondence; confidence in universal novelty is lower throughout.

  Lean links identify exact passages at commit
  <verbatim|3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464>. N1\UN6 refer to the
  six OpenAI preprints; their printed and PDF page numbers agree. Balaban
  citations use printed journal pages. Dimock citations use the preprint
  pagination of the dated versions specified in Ÿ7, which differs from
  journal pagination. The bibliography distinguishes full-text access with
  selective reading, partial extracts, abstracts and references cited only
  for established background.

  <subsection|1.2 Complete unit accounting><label|12-complete-unit-accounting>

  The units locate material; they do not imply that a declaration belongs
  mathematically to only one component. Within each unit, overlapping
  declaration spans count once. Of the declarations used by the main theorem,
  425 fall outside the units: 98 have usable source intervals and 327 do not.
  The intervals outside the units add 521 lines to the union. The older
  outline total, 413,085, is not the denominator for these percentages.

