  <section|2. The source toolkits><label|2-the-source-toolkits>

  <subsection|2.1 OpenAI's six papers><label|21-openais-six-papers>

  The papers are distinct and must not be treated as a single \PNLSM
  method\Q. The following descriptions concern their stated results and
  actual constructions; these descriptions do not certify those results.

  <subsubsection|N1: exponential decay, not an exact ultraviolet RG
  construction><label|n1-exponential-decay-not-an-exact-ultraviolet-rg-construction>

  Theorem 1.1, p.1, bounds the spin two-point function on every finite
  free-boundary subgraph of the square lattice, uniformly when ferromagnetic
  bond strengths are weakened or deleted. The result does not assert a full
  transfer gap; p.2 explicitly separates spin correlations from invariant
  observable sectors. Its distinctive analytic input is a fourth variation in
  two noncommuting rotation directions, <strong|Proposition 2.2>, pp.5\U8,
  Eq.(2.8). The finite-graph Ward identities permit arbitrary prescribed
  boundary spins. The commutator calculation is explicit: Eq.(2.10) is a
  change of variables, Eq.(2.11) gives the quadratic commutator field, and
  Eq.(2.12) cancels the fourth-order mixed coefficient for generator pattern
  <math|<around|(|a,b,a,b|)>>. The summed fourth derivative has a
  nonnegative-square contribution; it is not a perturbative effective-action
  calculation. Section 3, pp.9\U12, uses signed frequency families on annuli:
  the commutator response gains <math|<around|(|log N|)><rsup|2>>, whereas
  the error has size <math|log N>. The boundary twist partition-function
  ratio tends to one, uniformly under deleting bonds and vertices. Section 4
  converts this into rare embedded-Ising sign-cluster crossings. Simultaneous
  boundary pinning allows disjoint annuli to be compared independently; a
  coarse-path count then gives exponential decay. The final reduction from n
  components to three conditions the remaining coordinates and uses
  uniformity under weaker bonds. <strong|Provenance implication:> attributing
  the YM ultraviolet machinery to N1 on the basis of \Pnon-Abelian
  fluctuations\Q would be a superficial resemblance. The N2/N3
  retained-density constructions are the relevant comparators for that
  machinery.

  <subsubsection|N2: the principal direct comparator for the ultraviolet
  engine><label|n2-the-principal-direct-comparator-for-the-ultraviolet-engine>

  The result, Theorem 1.1, p.3, is a full lattice transfer-gap bound of order
  <math|<sqrt|\<beta\>>*<space|0.17em>e<rsup|-\<pi\>*\<beta\>>>. It is a
  lattice theorem; Ÿ11, p.81, separately discusses assumptions needed for a
  continuum spectral interpretation. Section 2, pp.9\U11, turns a small
  rectangular partition-function defect into a full transfer-gap estimate by
  positivity and time/space doubling. Section 3, pp.12\U33, proves
  preliminary all-observable mixing using quaternion current identities,
  stiffness reduction, disagreement propagation, and conditional
  Ising/rotator comparisons. This preliminary infrared input is logically
  separate from exact weak-coupling blocking. Section 4, pp.34\U38,
  constructs scalar block precisions, conditional means and covariances, and
  a positive edge-stack identity. Section 5, pp.38\U55, gives the exact
  nonlinear map, including rough configurations, and its comparison
  estimates. Section 6, pp.56\U61, fixes the kinetic drift through
  finite-volume coefficient comparisons. Section 7, pp.61\U65, admits
  trajectories and shoots to a fixed terminal kinetic coupling. Section 8,
  pp.65\U77, compares complete positive densities with signed mandatory
  covering expansions. Section 9, pp.77\U79, transfers a preliminary
  finite-box gap test from a fixed reference cutoff to all finer cutoffs.
  Section 10, pp.79\U81, obtains an upper mass bound from correlated block
  observables. Appendices A\UD supply the uniform free estimates, a fixed
  coefficient norm, complete supports of local sector factors, and joint
  Gaussian product majorants.

  The most useful distinctive devices for comparison are these:

  <\enumerate>
    <item><strong|Tagged normalized observation>, Eq.(5.2), p.39: use the
    normalized weighted spin mean on its good branch and a weighted
    constituent spin on its fallback branch; normalize the output noise
    exactly.

    <item><strong|Clipped-plus-masked-square kinetic energy>, Eq.(5.3), p.39:
    a quadratic direct term below threshold, a linear direct term above it,
    and a masked localized quadratic term.

    <item><strong|Fixed reference thresholds>, Eq.(5.1), p.38: all geometry
    uses <math|H<rsub|j>>, while the actual running coupling varies in a band
    around it.

    <item><strong|Complete read supports>, pp.39\U41 and Appendix C: record
    masks, eligibility tests, negative queries, windows, and lifted winding
    paths, not merely visible field arguments.

    <item><strong|Two activity classes>, Eqs.(5.7)\U(5.10), pp.40\U41:
    differentiable regular errors with zero affine Hessian; merely measurable
    signed large-gradient weights with nonempty inventories.

    <item><strong|Mandatory exact cover>, Eq.(5.9), p.40: disjoint supports
    must cover precisely the bad-edge set. This is not an ordinary optional
    polymer gas.

    <item><strong|One Gaussian for a fixed sector>, Eqs.(5.18)\U(5.21), p.44:
    window inverse columns, symmetrized finite-range covariance, explicit
    density-ratio and determinant residuals.

    <item><strong|Core reserves and protector supports>, Lemma 5.2,
    pp.43\U44; Appendix C: energy per primary seed pays entropy, bad-region
    normalization, and derivatives through hard inputs.

    <item><strong|Joint products before expansion>, Lemma 5.3, pp.47\U48;
    Appendix D: overlapping factors use the same Gaussian; endpoint quadratic
    loads are controlled collectively.

    <item><strong|Inventory-preserving connected reassembly>, Ÿ5.4, p.49:
    logarithm only for empty-inventory components; forbidden background
    polymers are opened and attached to seeded components.

    <item><strong|Exact kinetic reset>, Eqs.(5.40)\U(5.42), pp.54\U55: remove
    constant/affine Hessian pieces and put mask-complement corrections into
    the compulsory gas.

    <item><strong|Cutoff matching before comparison>, Ÿ7: the running
    marginal coordinate must agree before contracting all other endpoint
    data.
  </enumerate>

  <subsubsection|N3: extends the engine to continuum fields and stronger
  comparisons><label|n3-extends-the-engine-to-continuum-fields-and-stronger-comparisons>

  N3 directly states the inherited scalar and density ingredients in ŸŸ3\U4;
  Ÿ3.3, pp.23\U28, explicitly defines the adapted retained-density class. Its
  target is <math|S<rsup|2>>, not the quaternion group <math|S<rsup|3>>; this
  changes the rotation algebra, physical tangent dimension, Jacobian, and
  drift. Theorem 1.1, pp.5\U6, uses susceptibility and second-moment length
  to normalize the full continuum limit along all diverging real bare
  couplings. Theorem 1.2, pp.6\U7, first constructs the theory along a
  terminal-coupling trajectory. Section 2, pp.9\U21, proves preliminary
  mixing with a strict saving in <math|e<rsup|<around|(|4*\<pi\>-\<eta\>|)>*\<beta\>>>.
  Section 4, pp.29\U39, includes ordinary and inclined first observations,
  their free kernels, and common history estimates. Section 5, pp.39\U55,
  strengthens the regular contraction towards <math|L<rsup|-2>> using
  inversion/tangent-reflection cancellations. Its <strong|ambient
  completion>, Lemma 5.2, pp.42\U43, adjoins a normalized scalar Gaussian to
  the two-dimensional tangent field, producing an
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|3>>-valued common Gaussian without adding a physical
  degree of freedom. The scalar normalization cancels one color in the
  determinant, p.44. Sections 6\U7, pp.55\U69, shoot the terminal coupling
  and establish an <strong|integrated comparison> against one positive
  reference law. Proposition 7.1, p.61, allows the comparison activities to
  be signed or complex; only the base density is required to be positive and
  reflection positive. Lemmas 7.3\U7.4, pp.62\U65, combine chessboard
  exponential moments, local Lipschitz fillings of rough regions, cap
  entropy, and retained energy gain. The covering identities keep the
  original region geometry fixed during replacements, Ÿ7.4, p.65. Section 8,
  pp.69\U74, transfers a fixed-box estimate to a physical mass gap uniform in
  cutoff. Section 9, pp.74\U82, carries independent local complex spin
  sources; normalizing their aligned linear response gives the product of
  source-normalization factors. Sections 10\U11 construct Schwinger
  distributions, restore Euclidean rotations, reconstruct, and prove a
  separated nonzero fourth cumulant. Sections 12\U15 remove the bounded
  bare-offset ambiguity. The key additional ingredients are summable
  sensitivity of the kinetic error (Lemma 12.2, pp.97\U98) and two
  incommensurable offset periods arising from ordinary and inclined blockings
  (Ÿ15.2, pp.118\U119). <strong|Not transferable without argument:> these
  last uniqueness, mass-gap, and rotation mechanisms do not appear merely
  from recognizing the common ultraviolet density class.

  <subsubsection|N4: isolated mass from a massive continuum
  input><label|n4-isolated-mass-from-a-massive-continuum-input>

  Theorem 1.1, p.2, asserts a positive isolated lowest atom of the spin-field
  Källén\ULehmann measure. The paper takes the constructed O3 field and its
  cutoff-uniform physical gap and factorial moments as inputs; Proposition
  2.1, pp.4\U5, records them. Section 3, pp.6\U9, derives cylinder traces and
  torus-to-plane comparisons. Section 4, pp.9\U14, bounds covariance of even
  spin polynomials quadratically in spin two-point correlations, separating
  even and odd transfer sectors. Section 5, pp.14\U19, attaches
  quantitatively visible spin weight to low-energy states through embedded
  sign clusters and circuits. Section 6, pp.19\U21, obtains a
  linear-in-circumference state count below the two-excitation threshold
  using trace doubling and positive polynomial energy cutoffs. Section 7,
  pp.21\U25, combines momentum windows with the state count to prove that
  only finitely many masses lie near the bottom of spectral support. The
  proof does not obtain a particle pole from exponential decay alone.
  <strong|Provenance implication:> its use of positive spectral measures is
  standard shared machinery; its two-dimensional cluster/circuit/state-count
  mechanism is not established as a YM antecedent by the inspected Lean.

  <subsubsection|N5: an exact mass via a second
  regulator><label|n5-an-exact-mass-via-a-second-regulator>

  Theorem 1.1, p.3, states the exact prefactor <math|32*e<rsup|\<pi\>/4-1/2>>
  for the full O4 transfer gap in original lattice time units. Sections 2\U3,
  pp.6\U16, compare the spin-visible mass with the full spectrum and a
  rectangular partition-function rate. Sections 4\U6, pp.16\U55, introduce a
  Brownian U(2) regulator, a particle expansion, a commuting contact
  Hamiltonian, integral equations, finite-circle Bethe states and a spectral
  maximization over particle parities. These are distinctive integrability
  and regulator-specific steps; no basis was found for importing them into
  the YM comparison. Sections 7\U9, pp.55\U97, adapt the retained-density
  engine to four kinetic coefficients: spatial and temporal stiffnesses for
  each of the quaternion and circle fields, a real circle lift, complex
  weights, and integer curvature. The observation in Ÿ7.4, p.61, uses
  normalized mean/fallback noise on flat blocks and a medoid with randomized
  symmetric ties when curvature is present. Section 7.7, pp.65\U68, retains
  the same window Gaussian, determinant residuals and joint-factor logic.
  Section 9.2, pp.87\U88, shoots four parameters rather than a single
  coupling. Sections 9.3\U9.6 compare the possibly complex Brownian
  expression by integration against the positive flat reference measure,
  including growing rectangular aspect ratios. Section 10, pp.98\U104,
  determines a finite coupling shift by a helicity response, separately from
  the partition-error estimate. Section 11, pp.104\U107, extracts odd/even
  traces, removes the additional circle field, and transfers the auxiliary
  mass. <strong|Provenance implication:> the generalized exact-density engine
  is relevant; Bethe states, U(2) loops, winding-sector parity and exact mass
  extraction are not evidenced as ingredients of this Lean construction.

  <subsubsection|N6: XY uses a different mechanism><label|n6-xy-uses-a-different-mechanism>

  Theorem statements in Ÿ1, pp.2\U4, concern the critical spin field as an
  imaginary exponential of a Dirichlet GFF, normalized by exact center
  magnetization and a diagonal Green factor. Section 2, pp.6\U12, imports
  critical height and pin limits and relates spins to magnetic height
  sectors. Section 3, pp.12\U20, proves fixed-positive-radius-hole sector
  comparisons by Gaussian variational estimates. Section 4, pp.20\U24,
  controls flat pins on long annuli. Section 5, pp.24\U28, glues conditional
  interface kernels while summing the integer constant mode. Lemma 5.4, p.27,
  is a useful specific device: kernels in <math|<around|(|0,1|]>> with small
  average loss contract centered densities, so an arbitrarily poor but
  positive initial core eventually produces a last marginal bounded by two.
  Section 6, pp.28\U35, cancels identical microscopic annular normalizations
  against the center observable and obtains separated correlations. Section
  7, pp.35\U43, uses Lee\UYang moments, integrable two-point bounds,
  collision removal and local Sobolev tightness. <strong|Provenance
  implication:> this is not the retained-action/kinetic-reset construction of
  N2\UN3. Calling any annulus or Gaussian comparison in YM an XY adaptation
  would be superficial without further evidence.

  <subsection|2.2 Balaban's gauge toolkit><label|22-balabans-gauge-toolkit>

  Balaban's programme starts with compact-group link variables and preserves
  a complete effective density while integrating short scales. Its main
  analytic tasks are gauge fixing, regular localized propagators, nonlinear
  background fields, marginal extraction, and control of large-field regions
  through many iterations. The pertinent sources span 1983\U1989; they are
  not a single theorem that can be invoked as an unconditional
  four-dimensional construction.

  <strong|Block averaging and gauge fixing.> B-Prop-I ŸA, pp.18\U20,
  Eqs.(1.8)\U(1.17), gives contour averages and their linear versions after
  axial fixing. B-RG-I pp.252\U254, Eqs.(0.4)\U(0.15), uses route
  symmetrization, analytic nonlinear group means and gauge-invariant kernels.
  The mean respects inversion, permutation, left/right multiplication and
  linearization. B-Average pp.19\U20 Eqs.(10)\U(15), Proposition 3 p.36 and
  Proposition 7 p.43 directly develop nonlinear averages, quadratic
  remainders and analytic background dependence. B-Gauge Theorem 2 p.83 and
  Proposition 7 p.100 establish controlled axial/Landau gauge transfer and
  its inverse. Thus nonlinear averaging, gauge reconciliation and regular
  slices are older mechanisms; neither symmetry averaging nor a soft
  normalized kernel distinguishes N2 by itself. B-Prop-I ŸC, pp.20\U26,
  changes to a divergence-gauge formulation with projection operators to
  obtain regular propagators. The Lean comparison is
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/Patch.lean:102\U179>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/Patch.lean#L102-L179>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/Block.lean:49\U72>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/Block.lean#L49-L72>,
  and <hlink|<verbatim|YM/PCalc/Chart.lean:52\U67>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/PCalc/Chart.lean#L52-L67>;
  the formulas and division of gauge roles differ.

  <strong|Variational problem and backgrounds.> The nonlinear stationary
  background is built from a linear lift plus corrections satisfying local
  analytic equations. B-RG-II Ÿ1, pp.2\U3, Eqs.(1.2)\U(1.5), recalls those
  equations and localizes their propagators. B-Var directly supplies the
  gauge comparison: Theorem 1 p.279 gives the unique small critical orbit and
  regularity; Proposition 6 pp.295\U296 gives the analytic small solution;
  Proposition 9 p.309 gives dependence on coarse averages and external
  background, with localized derivative bounds (190), p.308. Its
  fixed-average constrained problem differs from the Lean soft-kernel
  problem. The derivative calculations preceding (189) are expressly omitted
  on p.308; this is a comparison of stated mechanisms, not certification of
  those estimates. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/PCalc/Chart.lean:78\U100>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/PCalc/Chart.lean#L78-L100>
  and <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Push/STATIONARY_UNIFORM/ImplicitRadius.lean:24\U43>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Push/STATIONARY_UNIFORM/ImplicitRadius.lean#L24-L43>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Push/STATIONARY_UNIFORM/ImplicitRadius.lean:80\U92>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Push/STATIONARY_UNIFORM/ImplicitRadius.lean#L80-L92>
  implement the corresponding task for the actual soft-block tree chart.

  <strong|Propagators and random walks.> B-Green Ÿ2, pp.574\U585, patches
  local inverses by generalized random-walk expansions. B-Prop-I ŸF,
  pp.35\U40, applies regularity/locality estimates to gauge propagators.
  B-Prop-II handles irregular domains and restrictions at several scales; see
  p.229 and Eq.(2.86), p.238. The traced Lean free rows instead arise from
  analytic symbols, Laurent coefficients and finite-period Fourier
  identities, <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Work/F07.lean:34\U81>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/F07.lean#L34-L81>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Work/F07.lean:109\U112>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/F07.lean#L109-L112>.
  B-Back Theorems 3.1\U3.4 pp.397\U400 supplies exponential decay,
  derivative/Hölder regularity and analytic background dependence; p.399
  makes the constants independent of the admissible domain sequence. The
  difference is implementation and required geometry: Balaban himself uses
  Fourier and Laurent formulas elsewhere.

  <strong|Small/large fields and localization.> B-Conv Ÿ2, pp.254\U262,
  represents complete densities by nested region histories with
  coupling-dependent cutoffs. B-Large-I pp.175\U177 explains why one
  rare-region penalty cannot simply be carried unchanged through arbitrarily
  many four-dimensional steps. Its R operation replaces exhausted large-field
  densities by normalized local ones, with quotient corrections preserving
  the integral, Eqs.(0.2)\U(0.6). B-Large-II Ÿ1 localizes and bounds these
  corrections. Lean's inspected interfaces retain forced inventories and
  explicit seed reserves, <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/Activities.lean:42\U55>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/Activities.lean#L42-L55>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/Activities.lean:113\U129>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/Activities.lean#L113-L129>
  and <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Pressure/Seeds.lean:34\U68>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Pressure/Seeds.lean#L34-L68>.
  The exact aged-region R replacement has not been identified as a separate
  step in that route. This is a bounded negative finding, not proof that no
  analogous local replacement occurs anywhere in the repository.

  <strong|Localization, polymers and marginal flow.> B-RG-II ŸŸ1\U2,
  pp.2\U22, makes nonlinear background dependence local and exponentiates
  connected corrections. B-RG-I p.258, Eqs.(0.28)\U(0.30), subtracts the
  gauge-action marginal direction. Its Ÿ4 differentiates gauge Ward
  identities through fourth order; Ÿ5, pp.292\U297, identifies the transverse
  Maxwell quadratic coefficient and higher-derivative remainder. The same
  gauge tasks appear at <hlink|<verbatim|YM/PCalc/Chart.lean:104\U109>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/PCalc/Chart.lean#L104-L109>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/Activities.lean:70\U75>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/Activities.lean#L70-L75>
  and <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/STATIONARY/Level/Transition.lean:86\U93>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/STATIONARY/Level/Transition.lean#L86-L93>.
  The particular compulsory-inventory gas and masked reset in Lean more
  closely match N2's later retained format.

  <strong|The precise ultraviolet endpoint.> B-Large-II Theorem 1, p.355,
  assumes all effective couplings lie in a sufficiently small interval. The
  next paragraph explicitly says that the argument removing the condition had
  not yet been published, so the theorem retains the assumption. Equation
  (0.1), pp.355\U356, provides upper and small-field lower density bounds
  uniform in scale, torus and configuration. The same page leaves
  physical-observable analysis, including loops, for further work. B-RG-I
  Theorem 2, p.259, states the tuned SU(2) running objective while postponing
  its proof. These scope limits are explicit in the sources; they are not
  inferred from the absence of a later reconstruction chapter. The Lean has
  its own coefficient calculation and transition proof,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/STATIONARY/FullPlanned.lean:17\U52>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/STATIONARY/FullPlanned.lean#L17-L52>,
  followed by observable and reconstruction work. None of this source
  comparison certifies that the latter work is mathematically correct.

  <strong|What the comparison does not identify as literally used.> The
  traced Lean route does not invoke Balaban's stability theorem as a black
  box. Its global nonlinear observation is a tagged soft kernel, not the hard
  block delta constraint of the initial linear Propagators formulation. Its
  free-row construction is not the same irregular multiscale-domain inverse
  construction. Its seed/activity interface is not demonstrably the same
  separate R replacement. Its stage schedule allows increasing orders and
  factors rather than simply fixing all such data once. The source pairs
  establishing these differences are given in components 04\U08 and 16\U17.
  They are departures from particular formulas, not evidence that localized
  integration, gauge symmetry or large-field control is new.

  <strong|Gaussian coordinates and Haar corrections.> B-UV3 pp.260\U262,
  Eqs.(17)\U(24), linearizes the nonlinear average, retains its Jacobian and
  the even Haar density, and separates Gaussian and nonlinear terms. Equation
  (63), p.272, localizes logarithmic determinants. Compare
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Ratio.lean:96\U105>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Ratio.lean#L96-L105>
  and <hlink|<verbatim|YM/AnalyticBridge/RELATIVE_LOGDET_TRACE/Sector.lean:54\U71>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/AnalyticBridge/RELATIVE_LOGDET_TRACE/Sector.lean#L54-L71>.
  These are direct older gauge antecedents. B-UV3 Theorem 1 p.257 is three
  dimensional and superrenormalizable; it does not supply the
  four-dimensional marginal coefficient transfer in
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/STATIONARY/Level/Transition.lean:153\U178>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/STATIONARY/Level/Transition.lean#L153-L178>.

  <subsection|2.3 Balaban's N-vector work, Dimock and common constructive
  tools><label|23-balabans-n-vector-work-dimock-and-common-constructive-tools>

  A Balaban-versus-NLSM opposition is historically misleading if \PBalaban\Q
  means only his gauge papers. B-RG-I p.260 already says the method also
  applies to two-dimensional group-valued chiral models. His later N-vector
  programme develops nonlinear backgrounds, RG equations, localization,
  fluctuation integration and large-field renormalization in a spin setting.
  B-N-II p.676 insists that localized outputs depend only on localized inputs
  and avoids artificial torus dependence by formulating the flow globally.
  B-N-Loc-I ŸŸ2\U4 treats backgrounds, covariances and nested nonlinear
  compositions. B-N-Large Ÿ4 treats a last-step generating functional.
  B-N-Corr p.493 and its main results concern spontaneous magnetization and
  transverse Goldstone behavior in dimensions at least three. These are not
  the two-dimensional massive O(3)/O(4) conclusions of N2\UN3, but they
  refute the idea that Balaban's interests stopped at vacuum partition
  functions.

  B-Corr pp.1\U2 presents a general effective-action method for correlations,
  with massive Gross\UNeveu as its worked example. It is neither a pure-gauge
  observable construction nor one of the classical N-vector papers despite
  its inclusion in the same bibliography. The bibliography also includes a
  Higgs lower-bound paper and Cammarota's polymer paper; neither should be
  misidentified as a Balaban pure-gauge article.

  Dimock I\UIII explain the Balaban-style small-field, large-field and
  convergence architecture for scalar <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4>> in three
  dimensions. They are excellent sources for Gaussian normalization, domains
  and quantifier order, but cannot supply missing non-Abelian identities. D-I
  preprint p.10, Eqs.(74),(78), exhibits determinant normalization; D-II
  preprint p.29 states a hierarchy of choices. D-III preprint p.33, Theorem 2
  and Corollary 1, establishes partition-function stability and remarks that
  correlations require further work.

  Bry ŸŸ3\U4, pp.138\U148, supplies older tree bounds and interpolation
  formulas common to both programmes. B-RG-I pp.250\U251 explicitly credits
  Gaw¦dzki\UKupiainen's analytic effective-action viewpoint. GK85 supplies a
  direct comparison. Its Ÿ4 p.212 Eq.(4.8) requires disjoint polymers to
  cover the bad region, with each touching a bad component; Ÿ5 pp.218\U221
  exponentiates the outside components and restores the same density class
  after marginal extraction. These are specific older antecedents for
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/Activities.lean:42\U55>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/Activities.lean#L42-L55>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/Activities.lean:113\U129>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/Activities.lean#L113-L129>
  and <hlink|<verbatim|YM/AnalyticBridge/VERIFIED_RESET_STEP/Evaluation.lean:71\U95>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/AnalyticBridge/VERIFIED_RESET_STEP/Evaluation.lean#L71-L95>,
  beyond generic polymer language. Its result is infrared asymptotic freedom
  of positive-coupling scalar lattice \<varphi\>\<#2074\>\<#2084\>, not a
  gauge UV theorem.

  BY90 pp.353\U356, Eqs.(1.7)\U(1.10), uses an exact disjoint-set polymer
  exponential/logarithm, explicitly distinguishing its algebra from GK's
  hybrid small/large-field representation. Theorems A/B pp.360\U363 extract
  local constant and quadratic gradient terms, shift the covariance and
  control regulator losses; Theorem B\<prime\> pp.383\U384 treats complex
  covariances. Its analytic norm already sums derivatives of all orders,
  p.359 Eq.(1.13). The Lean's masked, compulsory-inventory density is not
  literally BY's whole gas. These older constructions reduce the independent
  NLSM evidentiary weight of exact reblocking, analytic families, marginal
  subtraction and all-derivative control.

  <section|3. Component-by-component provenance><label|3-component-by-component-provenance>

  There are 39 components below, covering all 32 units. Large units are split
  by mechanism; several mechanisms cross unit boundaries.

  <subsection|01. Lattice, Haar measure and Wilson starting law \V
  <hlink|I.1|outline/units/I-1.tm>><label|01-lattice-haar-measure-and-wilson-starting-law--i1>

  <strong|Function and size.> 3,367 lines. This establishes periodic links,
  compact-group integration, plaquette energy, partition function and
  normalized lattice expectations. It is the exact finite-dimensional
  starting probability model.

  <strong|Evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Wilson/Law.lean:29>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Wilson/Law.lean#L29-L29>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Wilson/Law.lean:55\U99>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Wilson/Law.lean#L55-L99>
  defines energy, prior, bare density, partition and law. B-RG-I p.252,
  Eq.(0.2), starts with the same Wilson action and attributes it to Wilson.
  Wilson 1974, <em|Confinement of quarks>, is the original lattice-gauge
  reference; its broad role is cited from established knowledge, not a fresh
  full-paper reading.

  <strong|Classification.> STANDARD, high confidence; effectively 100% at the
  level of ideas assessed here. The abstract compact matrix group and formal
  symmetry bookkeeping are implementation generality. Sharing the starting
  model discriminates neither Balaban nor NLSM provenance.

  <subsection|02. Gauge-invariant local fields, jets and engineering degree
  \V <hlink|II.1|outline/units/II-1.tm>><label|02-gauge-invariant-local-fields-jets-and-engineering-degree--ii1>

  <strong|Function and size.> 10,793 lines. Finite jets of radial-gauge
  potentials provide a common algebra of local composite labels. A weighted
  degree makes each bounded-degree space finite dimensional and supports
  later quotient and detector arguments.

  <strong|Evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Labels/Jet.lean:19\U39>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Labels/Jet.lean#L19-L39>
  gives derivative weight and radial constraints.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Labels/Polynomial.lean:60\U112>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Labels/Polynomial.lean#L60-L112>
  gives degree, finite dimensionality, invariant space and labels. The
  closest broad precedent is the local composite-operator framework: W69 ŸII
  pp.1500\U1501 explicitly includes composite fields and is read in part; Z73
  remains abstract-only, as specified in Ÿ7. MRS93 p.329 distinguishes such
  renormalized physical composites from its elementary gauge-fixed field
  insertions. N3 Ÿ9.1, p.75, instead introduces complex sources for a
  particular spin field, not this gauge-jet algebra. B-RG-I Ÿ4, pp.281\U291,
  uses gauge Taylor identities but does not supply the all-label construction
  shown here.

  <strong|Classification.> Roughly STANDARD/ADAPTED-OTHER 75%, candidate-NEW
  organization 25%; medium confidence. The polynomial algebra is standard;
  the chosen common finite-dimensional system and its later compatibility
  requirements are specific to this construction. There is no evidence for an
  exclusive NLSM origin of the local-field concept.

  <subsection|03. Nilpotent markers and polymer/source algebra \V
  <hlink|II.2|outline/units/II-2.tm>><label|03-nilpotent-markers-and-polymersource-algebra--ii2>

  <strong|Function and size.> 3,613 lines. Truncated commuting source
  variables retain finitely many insertions and allow coefficient bounds for
  products and exponentials. This supplies bookkeeping shared by vacuum and
  observable expansions.

  <strong|Evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Marker/Truncated.lean:10\U58>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Marker/Truncated.lean#L10-L58>
  constructs nilpotence, finite exponential passage and coefficient budgets.
  N3 Ÿ9.1, p.75, likewise carries independent local complex sources through
  regular and covering lists. Bry ŸŸ3\U4, pp.138\U148, provides the older
  connected/polymer expansion setting; source differentiation is a standard
  extension of generating functions.

  <strong|Classification.> STANDARD 80%, ADAPTED-NLSM organizational affinity
  20%; high confidence for the standard part, medium for the immediate
  comparison. The Lean's finite algebra is not identical to an analytic
  complex-source neighborhood. Its value is exact coefficient tracking, not a
  new physical source principle.

  <subsection|04. Globally defined normalized block observation \V
  <hlink|III.1|outline/units/III-1.tm> (a)><label|04-globally-defined-normalized-block-observation--iii1-a>

  <strong|Function and size.> Within <hlink|III.1|outline/units/III-1.tm>'s
  4,133 lines. Gauge-covariant fine-link data produce coarse observations in
  every configuration, including rough ones. Tags choose frames and fallback
  predictors; compact-group noise is normalized exactly.

  <strong|Evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/Block.lean:39\U79>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/Block.lean#L39-L79>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/Block.lean:146\U163>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/Block.lean#L146-L163>
  defines connector, likelihood, noise, density and kernel normalization.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/Patch.lean:159\U179>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/Patch.lean#L159-L179>
  implements the mean/fallback predictor. N2 Ÿ5.1, p.39, Eq.(5.2), has the
  normalized mean on the good branch and a weighted constituent-spin fallback
  on the bad branch. B-RG-I pp.252\U254, Eqs.(0.4)\U(0.15), already has
  symmetric routes, nonlinear group means and gauge-invariant kernels. Fed87,
  pp.18\U20, also describes near-identity group averaging; its result is
  classical compatibility, not a quantum measure construction.

  <strong|Classification.> Approximately ADAPTED-NLSM 45%, ADAPTED-BALABAN
  40%, STANDARD 15%; medium-high confidence. The <strong|specific total
  tagged construction> is the close N2 match; normalized kernels or nonlinear
  means alone are not. Link transport between independently chosen frames is
  the substantive gauge adaptation missing from the spin formula.

  B-Average pp.19\U20 Eqs.(10)\U(15), Proposition 3 p.36 and Proposition 7
  p.43 directly strengthen the nonlinear analytic-average antecedent. They
  concern a local logarithmic average; <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/Patch.lean:169\U179>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/Patch.lean#L169-L179>
  supplies the different total minimizing/fallback predictor. Confidence
  remains high in this concrete distinction, medium in the relative
  attribution of the whole observation.

  <subsection|05. Symmetric frame selection and integration gauges \V
  <hlink|III.1|outline/units/III-1.tm> (b)><label|05-symmetric-frame-selection-and-integration-gauges--iii1-b>

  <strong|Function and size.> Also within
  <hlink|III.1|outline/units/III-1.tm>. A symmetric frame selector belongs to
  the observation, whereas bounded-height tree slices supply coordinates for
  integration. Keeping these roles distinct matters: a minimizing observation
  frame is not automatically the Gaussian integration gauge.

  <strong|Evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/Patch.lean:109\U155>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/Patch.lean#L109-L155>
  defines the minimizing regime, eligible frames and their masses.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Tree/Pines.lean:7\U12>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Tree/Pines.lean#L7-L12>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Tree/Pines.lean:24\U29>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Tree/Pines.lean#L24-L29>
  explicitly separates the minimizing frame and arbitrary rooted forests.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/PCalc/Chart.lean:52\U67>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/PCalc/Chart.lean#L52-L67>
  uses Lie-algebra coordinates on non-tree links. B-RG-I pp.253\U254,
  Eqs.(0.11),(0.14), symmetrizes axial fixing and uses exponential gauge
  factors. B-Prop-I ŸC, pp.20\U26, Eqs.(1.22)\U(1.47), performs a different
  divergence-gauge change with projections.

  <strong|Classification.> Predominantly ADAPTED-BALABAN and STANDARD for
  controlled gauge transfer, with a specialized observation/forest
  implementation; high confidence in the older mechanism, medium in the
  implementation comparison. B-Gauge Theorem 2 p.83, Eqs.(1.36)\U(1.39),
  gives a unique small axial-to-Landau transformation with regularity bounds;
  Proposition 7 p.100, Eqs.(1.139)\U(1.145), gives the reverse
  correspondence. Generic \Pgauge synchronization\Q should therefore not
  carry candidate-new credit. The narrower distinction is reconciling this
  particular minimizing/fallback observation with an independently chosen
  bounded-height forest. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Tree/Pines.lean:7\U12>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Tree/Pines.lean#L7-L12>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Tree/Pines.lean:24\U29>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Tree/Pines.lean#L24-L29>
  separates them; <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Work/F04/Choices.lean:78\U86>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/F04/Choices.lean#L78-L86>
  compares tested germs under the branch hypotheses. No literal use of
  Balaban's complete gauge-change sequence is established by these passages.

  <subsection|06. Reference schedule, constant order and terminal shooting \V
  <hlink|III.2|outline/units/III-2.tm>><label|06-reference-schedule-constant-order-and-terminal-shooting--iii2>

  <strong|Function and size.> 18,129 lines. A reference inverse coupling
  determines thresholds and localization radii while the actual coupling is
  varied. Successive finite stages accommodate increasing derivative orders
  and choose a trajectory reaching a prescribed terminal band.

  <strong|Evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/History.lean:43\U118>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/History.lean#L43-L118>
  separates stage choices, hatted reference couplings, geometry and schedule
  conditions. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Shoot/Reach.lean:18\U49>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Shoot/Reach.lean#L18-L49>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Shoot/Reach.lean:112\U140>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Shoot/Reach.lean#L112-L140>
  uses surviving intervals and continuity to shoot the trajectory. N2
  Eq.(5.1), p.38, and Ÿ7, pp.61\U65, have the same fixed-reference geometry
  and terminal matching purpose. BBS19 Ÿ8.3, preprint pp.122\U123,
  Proposition 8.3.1, provides an older surviving-interval construction of
  critical trajectories. D-II Theorem 3.1, preprint p.29, makes an older
  constructive order of choices explicit.

  <strong|Classification.> ADAPTED-NLSM 40%, STANDARD/ADAPTED-OTHER 40%,
  candidate-NEW growing-order organization 20%; medium-high confidence. N2
  fixes a block factor and finite order; Lean lets factors/orders grow under
  one common run. The threshold monitor is <math|e<rsup|<around|(|log log
  \<beta\>|)><rsup|2>>>, not N2's fixed power of <math|log H>. Neither
  shooting by continuity nor ordering constants is itself new.

  GK85 p.211 Eq.(4.1) uses the numerical reference <math|n<rsub|0>+n> for
  small-field domains, p.221 Eq.(6.2) bounds the running coupling by
  inverse-reference envelopes, p.222 orders the constants, and p.246 uses
  nested intervals to tune the critical mass. MRS93 p.330
  Eqs.(II.11)\U(II.12) distinguishes tentative and exact effective couplings.
  BY90 pp.360\U363 controls successive regulator losses with fixed block
  size. Thus reference envelopes, tuning intervals and constant schedules are
  older. N2 is closest to the particular terminal-coupling arrangement, while
  Lean's increasing stages and common gauge-composite obligations remain a
  specialized extension. Confidence in that closest-source ranking is medium;
  none of the older scalar couplings has the gauge beta-function
  normalization.

  <subsection|07. Free quadratic action, covariances and localized rows \V
  <hlink|III.3|outline/units/III-3.tm>><label|07-free-quadratic-action-covariances-and-localized-rows--iii3>

  <strong|Function and size.> 8,966 lines. This constructs the free gauge
  reference, stationary lifts, square-root rows and exponential locality
  uniformly in finite period. These are the linear estimates needed before
  the nonlinear background can be controlled.

  <strong|Evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/PREC/TreeMatch.lean:76\U103>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/PREC/TreeMatch.lean#L76-L103>
  gives the coarse quadratic square-completion identity.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Work/F07.lean:34\U81>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/F07.lean#L34-L81>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Work/F07.lean:109\U112>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/F07.lean#L109-L112>
  constructs analytic-collar roots and all-period Fourier identities.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/FREE_KERNELS/Ground.lean:54\U59>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/FREE_KERNELS/Ground.lean#L54-L59>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/FREE_KERNELS/Ground.lean:69\U120>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/FREE_KERNELS/Ground.lean#L69-L120>
  identifies the concrete rows and uniform bounds. B-Prop-I ŸD\UF, pp.26\U40,
  especially Eq.(1.64)\U(1.67), has the stationary Gaussian lift and
  comparison to Maxwell energy. B-Green Ÿ2, pp.574\U585, and B-Prop-II ŸB,
  p.229, use generalized random walks to patch local inverses on irregular
  multiscale domains. N2 Ÿ4, pp.34\U38, gives scalar block precisions and
  covariances, a useful but less gauge-specific comparator.

  <strong|Classification.> ADAPTED-BALABAN 45%, STANDARD spectral/Gaussian
  tools 40%, later adaptation 15%; high confidence in the mechanism
  distinction. The Lean's translation-invariant row construction is
  Fourier/Laurent based, with sector restrictions handled later. Balaban also
  uses Fourier and Laurent methods; the difference is the construction's
  organization and domains, not possession of Fourier analysis.

  The direct regular-background antecedent is B-Back Theorems 3.1\U3.3
  pp.397\U399 and Theorem 3.4 p.400: exponential decay, derivative/Hölder
  bounds and analytic continuation, with constants independent of admissible
  nested domains (p.399). This directly supports the Balaban
  background-regularity attribution; it leaves the Fourier/Laurent versus
  domain-local random-walk organization distinct.

  <subsection|08. Sector change and localized Gaussian covariance \V
  <hlink|III.4|outline/units/III-4.tm> (a)><label|08-sector-change-and-localized-gaussian-covariance--iii4-a>

  <strong|Function and size.> Within <hlink|III.4|outline/units/III-4.tm>'s
  37,825 lines. The exact integral is rewritten in tree-chart coordinates,
  shifted around a background and expressed against a localized Gaussian.
  Masks, tags, inactive variables and residual quadratic terms remain in the
  equality.

  <strong|Evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Change.lean:63\U71>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Change.lean#L63-L71>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Change.lean:95\U103>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Change.lean#L95-L103>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Change.lean:125\U135>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Change.lean#L125-L135>
  records the actual retained factors and integral identity.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Windows.lean:65\U74>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Windows.lean#L65-L74>
  builds inverse columns on separate windows, then symmetrizes them. N2 p.44,
  Eqs.(5.18)\U(5.21), uses exactly this window-column/symmetrization
  arrangement and retains the inverse defect in the density ratio. B-RG-II
  p.12, Eq.(2.5), is an older Gaussian-conditioning/localization predecessor,
  but less close to this exact finite-range covariance formula.

  <strong|Classification.> ADAPTED-NLSM 55%, STANDARD Gaussian identities
  25%, ADAPTED-BALABAN 20%; high confidence. The symmetrized matrix is
  <strong|not> asserted to be the true principal inverse; the residual is
  essential. Gauge null directions, active non-tree links and regions held
  fixed require new model-specific estimates. N3's ambient normal scalar
  completion, pp.42\U44, differs from the inactive-coordinate padding in
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Completion.lean:34\U45>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Completion.lean#L34-L45>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Completion.lean:149\U180>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Completion.lean#L149-L180>.

  B-UV3 pp.260\U262, Eqs.(17)\U(24), supplies a direct older
  nonlinear-coordinate change, Jacobian and Gaussian fluctuation
  decomposition. It strengthens the Balaban part of the comparison. The close
  N2 match is specifically the separate inverse-window columns and their
  symmetrization with a retained defect, not Gaussian change of variables or
  background expansion in isolation.

  <subsection|09. Local determinant ratios, Jacobians and joint Gaussian
  bounds \V <hlink|III.4|outline/units/III-4.tm>
  (b)><label|09-local-determinant-ratios-jacobians-and-joint-gaussian-bounds--iii4-b>

  <strong|Function and size.> Also within
  <hlink|III.4|outline/units/III-4.tm>. Log determinants and inverse defects
  are decomposed into localized chains; several factors are bounded under one
  Gaussian before regrouping. The Haar chart contributes an even,
  quadratic-order Jacobian correction.

  <strong|Evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/AnalyticBridge/RELATIVE_LOGDET_TRACE/Sector.lean:54\U71>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/AnalyticBridge/RELATIVE_LOGDET_TRACE/Sector.lean#L54-L71>
  retains both local determinant data and residuals.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/AnalyticBridge/ANNULUS_RATIO_INSERTIONS/Arc.lean:121\U166>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/AnalyticBridge/ANNULUS_RATIO_INSERTIONS/Arc.lean#L121-L166>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/AnalyticBridge/ANNULUS_RATIO_INSERTIONS/Arc.lean:176\U201>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/AnalyticBridge/ANNULUS_RATIO_INSERTIONS/Arc.lean#L176-L201>
  combines quadratic loads, polynomial insertions and rarity under a common
  law. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Ratio.lean:96\U105>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Ratio.lean#L96-L105>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Ratio.lean:125\U145>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Ratio.lean#L125-L145>
  establishes the quadratic Jacobian error. N2 p.44, Eqs.(5.19)\U(5.21),
  Lemma 5.3, pp.47\U48, and Appendix D provide the closest combined
  formulation. B-Large-II pp.356\U358, Eqs.(1.2),(1.10), already retains
  determinant normalization; D-I preprint p.10, Eqs.(74),(78), displays the
  familiar determinant factor.

  <strong|Classification.> ADAPTED-NLSM 45%, STANDARD 40%, ADAPTED-BALABAN
  15%; medium-high confidence. The same-Gaussian treatment of overlapping
  factors is the specific affinity; ordinary determinant calculus and Haar
  inversion symmetry are standard. Treating overlapping factors as
  independent would lose the actual content of the comparison.

  B-UV3 p.260 explicitly identifies the Haar density as analytic, positive
  and even; p.261 Eqs.(19)\U(22) retains the nonlinear Jacobian, and p.272
  Eq.(63) localizes the log determinant. These are direct gauge antecedents
  for the Jacobian and determinant pieces. BY90 Theorem B p.362 and Ÿ8
  pp.378\U381 also bounds Gaussian integration of interacting local
  functionals collectively. The N2 affinity remains the particular
  inserted-factor estimates in its retained-density setting, not a claim that
  joint Gaussian control first appeared there.

  <subsection|10. Absolute polymer sums and connected logarithms \V
  <hlink|III.5|outline/units/III-5.tm>><label|10-absolute-polymer-sums-and-connected-logarithms--iii5>

  <strong|Function and size.> 9,671 lines. Connected graph sums are bounded
  by trees, summed with support weights, and exponentiated back to the gas.
  Absolute convergence supplies marked-site and tail estimates usable in
  later source constructions.

  <strong|Evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Push/ABSOLUTE_SUMS/Graph.lean:113\U148>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Push/ABSOLUTE_SUMS/Graph.lean#L113-L148>
  controls graph sums by a rooted-tree organization.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Push/ABSOLUTE_SUMS/Log.lean:114\U149>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Push/ABSOLUTE_SUMS/Log.lean#L114-L149>
  proves the logarithm/exponential identity.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Push/ABSOLUTE_SUMS/Clusters.lean:224\U243>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Push/ABSOLUTE_SUMS/Clusters.lean#L224-L243>
  supplies the geometric connected-cluster tail. Bry Ÿ3, pp.138\U143, Theorem
  3.1 and Corollary 3.2, is the direct older tree-control reference; Cam Ÿ3,
  pp.525\U528, covers polymer convergence. B-RG-II Ÿ2, pp.11\U22, and N2
  Ÿ5.4, p.49, both use this general constructive machinery.

  <strong|Classification.> STANDARD/ADAPTED-OTHER about 85%,
  application-specific support format 15%; high confidence. Counting the
  whole unit as exclusively Balaban or exclusively NLSM would confuse use of
  a method with its origin. The special exact inventory algebra belongs to
  the surrounding representation, not to the tree inequality itself.

  BY90 pp.354\U355 Eqs.(1.7)\U(1.9) gives the disjoint-set algebra and finite
  polymer exponential/logarithm; pp.364\U366 Eqs.(2.1)\U(2.10) gives
  relevant-term extraction and connected reblocking. GK85 p.218 Eq.(5.28)
  gives the connected-graph exponentiation in a hybrid representation. These
  direct older formulas reinforce the STANDARD/ADAPTED-OTHER classification;
  BY's algebraic logarithm should not be conflated with an ordinary
  convergent logarithm on every field configuration.

  <subsection|11. Stationary nonlinear background with a uniform radius \V
  <hlink|III.6|outline/units/III-6.tm>><label|11-stationary-nonlinear-background-with-a-uniform-radius--iii6>

  <strong|Function and size.> 19,698 lines. A critical branch for the actual
  classical action is constructed in gauge-fixed coordinates. The subsequent
  estimates make its analytic radius and inverse-Hessian control usable
  uniformly in volume.

  <strong|Evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/PCalc/Chart.lean:78\U100>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/PCalc/Chart.lean#L78-L100>
  defines the tested action, critical branch and Hessian condition.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Work/F04.lean:62\U86>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/F04.lean#L62-L86>
  constructs the local branch by an implicit-function argument.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Push/STATIONARY_UNIFORM/ImplicitRadius.lean:24\U43>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Push/STATIONARY_UNIFORM/ImplicitRadius.lean#L24-L43>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Push/STATIONARY_UNIFORM/ImplicitRadius.lean:80\U92>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Push/STATIONARY_UNIFORM/ImplicitRadius.lean#L80-L92>
  prices an inverse without a spurious volume factor from changing norms.
  B-RG-II Ÿ1, pp.2\U3, Eqs.(1.2)\U(1.5), recalls the nonlinear background
  equations and their propagator dependence. B-Var Theorem 1 p.279,
  Proposition 6 pp.295\U296 and Proposition 9 p.309 directly supply the gauge
  antecedent: a unique small critical orbit, an analytic stationary solution,
  and regularity/localized dependence on the background. Equation (190),
  p.308, states derivative decay, although its preceding detailed derivative
  calculation is omitted there. N2 Ÿ5.2, pp.42\U44, uses a localized affine
  Gaussian shift with an explicit residual; this is a related but weaker role
  than solving nonlinear stationarity.

  <strong|Classification.> Predominantly ADAPTED-BALABAN with STANDARD
  analytic tools and a specialized soft-kernel implementation; high
  confidence in the direct local-branch and regularity antecedent. Generic
  analytic stationary branches and background regularity are established
  older mechanisms. The branch is for a soft penalty on tree-chart variables,
  not literally Balaban's constrained minimizer on nested irregular domains.
  The implicit-function theorem alone is routine; uniformity in the
  appropriate norms is the substantive work.

  <subsection|12. Matching the Wilson energy through fourth order \V
  <hlink|III.7|outline/units/III-7.tm> (a)><label|12-matching-the-wilson-energy-through-fourth-order--iii7-a>

  <strong|Function and size.> Within <hlink|III.7|outline/units/III-7.tm>'s
  19,927 lines. The chosen coarse action reproduces the relevant low Taylor
  coefficients and separates the marginal curvature-square direction from
  irrelevant terms. This is where gauge symmetry and the explicit
  cubic/quartic structures matter.

  <strong|Evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/PCalc/Chart.lean:104\U109>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/PCalc/Chart.lean#L104-L109>
  defines <hlink|<verbatim|SameJet>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/PCalc/Chart.lean#L104-L109>
  and <hlink|<verbatim|ClassicalMatch>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/PCalc/Chart.lean#L104-L109>
  through order four. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/PathActions.lean:236\U242>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/PathActions.lean#L236-L242>
  retains principal, cubic, quartic and loop terms.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/STATIONARY/Level/Transition.lean:86\U93>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/STATIONARY/Level/Transition.lean#L86-L93>
  packages matching with the quantitative transition. B-RG-I Ÿ4, pp.281\U291,
  differentiates gauge invariance through fourth order; Ÿ5, pp.292\U297,
  extracts the marginal transverse Maxwell form. N2 Eq.(5.7), p.40, and
  Eq.(5.40), p.54, impose the later activity-class affine-Hessian
  subtraction.

  <strong|Classification.> ADAPTED-BALABAN 55%, STANDARD gauge power counting
  25%, ADAPTED-NLSM format 20%; high confidence. Zero affine Hessian is not a
  distinctive NLSM invention: the gauge-specific subtraction has a direct
  Balaban antecedent. The exact path-based retained format still requires a
  separate preservation proof in Lean.

  B-Var ŸG pp.305\U307, Eqs.(174)\U(180), expands the stationary background
  around its linear lift; B-UV3 p.261 Eq.(19) separates quadratic and higher
  action terms. These strengthen the background-Taylor antecedent. Neither
  alone identifies the four-dimensional marginal coefficient: B-RG-I ŸŸ4\U5
  remains the direct gauge symmetry/marginal comparator for
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/STATIONARY/Level/Transition.lean:86\U93>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/STATIONARY/Level/Transition.lean#L86-L93>.

  <subsection|13. Numerical one-loop slope and constructive matching \V
  <hlink|III.7|outline/units/III-7.tm> (b)><label|13-numerical-one-loop-slope-and-constructive-matching--iii7-b>

  <strong|Function and size.> Also within
  <hlink|III.7|outline/units/III-7.tm>. A vector/ghost calculation identifies
  the universal logarithmic coefficient, and a bounded-error comparison
  attaches it to the actual blocked action. These are different obligations:
  computing a continuum loop integral does not by itself identify the exact
