  derivative/support costs fit their budgets. This closes the retained
  density class beyond a formal perturbation series.

  <strong|Evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG08/Steam/Routing.lean:14\U37>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG08/Steam/Routing.lean#L14-L37>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG08/Steam/Routing.lean:85\U91>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG08/Steam/Routing.lean#L85-L91>
  makes the correction order triangular in coupling degree and field rank.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/Activities.lean:98\U111>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/Activities.lean#L98-L111>
  states the regular/forced bounds that closure must preserve. N2 Theorem
  5.1, pp.41\U42, ŸŸ5.3\U5.6 and Eq.(5.43), p.55, gives the closest
  exact-step closure architecture. N3 Ÿ5, pp.39\U55, strengthens contractions
  with reflection/inversion cancellations. B-RG-I p.258, Eqs.(0.28)\U(0.30),
  and B-RG-II Ÿ2 provide older summable irrelevant remainders and
  localization/exponentiation.

  <strong|Classification.> ADAPTED-NLSM 45%, ADAPTED-BALABAN 25%, STANDARD
  estimates 15%, candidate-NEW uniform extension 15%; medium confidence. The
  same representation does not make the gauge estimates automatic: cubic
  vertices, gauge degeneracy, color and four-dimensional marginality must be
  handled. The inspected interfaces support architectural attribution; they
  do not constitute a line-by-line proof of every closure inequality.

  GK85 ŸŸ6,9 pp.221\U222,233\U237 and BY90 Theorems A/B pp.360\U363 already
  close local analytic activity classes after marginal extraction and bound
  regulator losses. MRS93 ŸŸVI\UVII pp.368\U376 treats gauge background
  determinants, protected large fields and coupling-flow remainders. Thus
  closing a gauge RG with a marginal direction is not a defensible broad
  novelty claim; only the controlled realization of this particular retained
  transformation remains a candidate, with medium-low novelty confidence.

  <subsection|18. Volume-uniform normalization and cancellation of gauge
  powers \V <hlink|III.11|outline/units/III-11.tm>
  (a)><label|18-volume-uniform-normalization-and-cancellation-of-gauge-powers--iii11-a>

  <strong|Function and size.> Within <hlink|III.11|outline/units/III-11.tm>'s
  42,556 lines. A Haar-shell lower bound and triangular plaquette upper bound
  control normalized insertions uniformly in torus size. The key
  normalization cancels extensive powers of the small coupling before a
  chessboard root is taken. The unrooted estimate still has an exponential
  volume cost; the uniform statement is the resulting per-cell/root price.

  <strong|Evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/RANK/Determinant.lean:76\U111>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/RANK/Determinant.lean#L76-L111>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/RANK/Determinant.lean:189\U217>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/RANK/Determinant.lean#L189-L217>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/RANK/Determinant.lean:240\U274>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/RANK/Determinant.lean#L240-L274>
  uses exponents <math|3*V+1> and <math|3*V-3>, leaving a global
  <math|g<rsup|-4*dim G>> factor. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/RANK/StationMeter.lean:8\U14>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/RANK/StationMeter.lean#L8-L14>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/RANK/StationMeter.lean:28\U44>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/RANK/StationMeter.lean#L28-L44>
  states the per-cell price without an extensive logarithmic loss.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/RANK/Stamp.lean:131\U167>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/RANK/Stamp.lean#L131-L167>
  converts the ratio to the root bound. B-Large-II pp.356\U359,
  Eqs.(1.1)\U(1.10), has determinant-sensitive local normalization ratios, an
  older general task. N3 Proposition 7.1 and Lemmas 7.3\U7.4, pp.61\U65,
  instead compare through rough-region fillings against a positive reference
  law.

  <strong|Classification.> STANDARD integration with substantial
  ADAPTED-BALABAN/ADAPTED-OTHER normalization antecedents; high confidence in
  the older general task, low confidence in novelty of the particular torus
  estimate. <strong|No specific NLSM adaptation is established for this
  cancellation proof.> The direct source check rules out assigning all of
  <hlink|III.11|outline/units/III-11.tm> to N3 merely because both contain
  integrated comparison estimates.

  MRS93 p.368 Eq.(VI.1) combines the gauge/ghost determinant ratio,
  counterterms and background action; Lemma VI.1 p.369, Eq.(VI.5), bounds the
  combined large-field dressing factor by one for its stabilizing cutoff. It
  explicitly explains why a cruder per-volume bound would not suffice. This
  substantially strengthens ADAPTED-OTHER antecedents for the normalization
  problem. Seiler82 pp.118\U121 instead treats matter determinants in a
  prescribed regular external gauge field; the four-dimensional statement is
  explicitly Quasi-Theorem 6.10, not a pure-gauge measure result.

  The exact <math|3*V+1> versus <math|3*V-3> sweep/root estimate is unmatched
  in the targeted passages, but remains a low-confidence novelty candidate.
  The first count is ordinary spanning-tree arithmetic; possible substantive
  novelty lies in the uniform numerator/lower-bound estimates and their use
  for composite insertions.

  <subsection|19. Interacting/free rank and finite detector comparison \V
  <hlink|III.11|outline/units/III-11.tm> (b)><label|19-interactingfree-rank-and-finite-detector-comparison--iii11-b>

  <strong|Function and size.> Also within
  <hlink|III.11|outline/units/III-11.tm>. Finite test systems compare
  interacting composite labels with the free Maxwell quotient, control
  equation terms and stabilize the field-space rank. Good-scale estimates
  permit corrected trial systems and coherent choices of coordinates.

  <strong|Evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/MergeProof/Powder.lean:19\U71>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/MergeProof/Powder.lean#L19-L71>
  combines equation-count savings with Maxwell rank data.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SharpGram/Weak/Compatible.lean:140\U159>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SharpGram/Weak/Compatible.lean#L140-L159>
  gives compatible changes of coordinates across degrees. N3 Ÿ9, pp.74\U82,
  normalizes spin sources, and Ÿ11.3, pp.93\U94, compares a specific spin
  cumulant; neither is the all-composite Maxwell rank argument. B-RG-I Ÿ4,
  pp.281\U291, controls gauge Taylor constraints, without the continuum
  label-identification endpoint displayed here.

  <strong|Classification.> STANDARD finite-dimensional stability 45%,
  ADAPTED-OTHER comparison strategy 15%, candidate-NEW simultaneous
  detector/rank construction 40%; medium confidence. The potentially new
  content is the set of uniform inputs and their coherent use, not the
  elementary fact that invertibility survives small perturbations. A deeper
  comparison with constructive composite-operator literature is a priority
  before claiming originality across the literature.

  MRS93 ŸVIII pp.377\U378 supplies an arbitrary-order gauge-fixed
  elementary-source hierarchy, but p.329 explicitly leaves construction of
  renormalized physical composite operators outside the paper. It therefore
  strengthens the old source-analysis context without supplying the Maxwell
  equation-quotient and simultaneous finite-detector comparison at
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/MergeProof/Powder.lean:19\U71>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/MergeProof/Powder.lean#L19-L71>.
  The latter remains a bounded candidate; neither all insertions nor rank
  stability alone is new.

  <subsection|20. Reflection positivity, chessboard roots and derivative
  budgets \V <hlink|III.12|outline/units/III-12.tm>
  (a)><label|20-reflection-positivity-chessboard-roots-and-derivative-budgets--iii12-a>

  <strong|Function and size.> Within <hlink|III.12|outline/units/III-12.tm>'s
  18,944 lines. Reflection positivity yields a seminorm controlling
  disseminated observables. Gauge-invariant local fields and their finite
  differences receive volume-uniform bounds suitable for continuum
  extraction.

  <strong|Evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Cells/Certificate.lean:14\U24>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Cells/Certificate.lean#L14-L24>
  packages reflected positivity, product dissemination and the root triangle
  inequality. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Extraction/Center.lean:21\U23>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Extraction/Center.lean#L21-L23>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Extraction/Center.lean:38\U40>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Extraction/Center.lean#L38-L40>
  states the centered observable budget. FILS78 Ÿ4, preprint pp.33\U36,
  Theorems 4.1\U4.3, is the direct older source for abstract and torus
  chessboard estimates. N3 Lemma 7.3, p.62, uses chessboard exponential
  moments for kinetic rows; N2 ŸŸ2,9 uses reflection positivity for a
  distinct partition-doubling mass-gap argument.

  <strong|Classification.> STANDARD/ADAPTED-OTHER 65%, ADAPTED-NLSM
  source-estimate affinity 15%, candidate-NEW all-label derivative
  application 20%; high confidence in the base inequality, medium in the
  adaptation. The same positivity principle has different downstream uses.
  Here a root bound and derivative control must not be relabelled a full
  transfer-gap estimate.

  Seiler82 Ÿ2a pp.14\U16 directly proves lattice-gauge reflection positivity
  (Theorem 2.1), the quotient Hilbert-space construction and transfer
  contraction, then states the chessboard inequality (Theorem 2.2). Its
  displayed chessboard formulation uses local matter functions and refers the
  gauge-field extension to another source. This directly strengthens the
  older lattice-gauge setting; the general chessboard comparison also rests
  on FILS. Osterwalder\USeiler 1978 remains unavailable in full.

  <subsection|21. Fixed-volume convergence and analytic tuning \V
  <hlink|III.12|outline/units/III-12.tm> (b)><label|21-fixed-volume-convergence-and-analytic-tuning--iii12-b>

  <strong|Function and size.> Also within
  <hlink|III.12|outline/units/III-12.tm>. Fixed-level transfer data give
  Cauchy correlations at a fixed physical period, first on convenient probes
  and then generally. Analytic coupling families and a common successful
  recursion constrain tuning without asserting uniqueness of all possible
  continuum theories.

  <strong|Evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/FullVolume/Gables/Vendee.lean:44\U61>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/FullVolume/Gables/Vendee.lean#L44-L61>
  gives the run and coefficient convergence.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/FullVolume/Fields/Capital.lean:55\U61>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/FullVolume/Fields/Capital.lean#L55-L61>
  states the Cauchy/confluence step. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/FullVolume/Tuned.lean:58\U72>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/FullVolume/Tuned.lean#L58-L72>
  passes from dense probes and equicontinuity to the general limit.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/FullVolume/Finale/Program.lean:60\U77>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/FullVolume/Finale/Program.lean#L60-L77>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/FullVolume/Finale/Program.lean:133\U158>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/FullVolume/Finale/Program.lean#L133-L158>
  records the successful-recursion and analytic-family scope. N3 Proposition
  10.2, pp.84\U86, likewise obtains fixed-torus source convergence from
  coarse-level comparisons. N3 ŸŸ12\U15, pp.95\U120, goes further by removing
  bare-offset ambiguity with additional comparisons.

  <strong|Classification.> STANDARD limit/analytic arguments 45%,
  ADAPTED-NLSM construction affinity 30%, specialized extension 25%; medium
  confidence. Lean's fixed-volume full-mesh convergence does not imply a
  unique infinite-volume limit. The N3 inclined-block and
  incommensurable-period mechanism has not been identified as the tuning
  mechanism here.

  MRS93 pp.327\U330 already states a fixed-IR ultraviolet Schwinger-function
  limit and compares tentative/exact coupling schedules. BY90 p.364 proves
  analyticity under RG iteration and of the pressure. These strengthen the
  older construction/analyticity context, without supplying the Lean tuned
  composite full-mesh Cauchy statement. Seiler82 pp.100\U101 distinguishes
  existence of scaling limits from independence of the chosen physical
  normalization; neither fixed-volume convergence nor analytic tuning alone
  establishes universality.

  <subsection|22. Extension to all labels and all finite orders \V
  <hlink|III.13|outline/units/III-13.tm>><label|22-extension-to-all-labels-and-all-finite-orders--iii13>

  <strong|Function and size.> 22,077 lines. One family must serve every
  finite degree and requested derivative order, with compatible labels,
  budgets and comparisons. The construction is selected once rather than
  separately for each later observable.

  <strong|Evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SharpGram/Assembly/Flowers.lean:75\U130>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SharpGram/Assembly/Flowers.lean#L75-L130>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|uv_log>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SharpGram/Assembly/Flowers.lean#L75-L130>,
  constructs the common family while stages increase their orders.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Source/Construction.lean:24\U62>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Source/Construction.lean#L24-L62>
  makes the degree factory, stages and normalized construction explicit. N3
  Ÿ9.1\U9.2, pp.75\U80, already extends the density flow to local spin
  sources and proves fixed-layer convergence. B-N-Large Ÿ4 and B-N-Corr p.493
  establish that generating functionals and correlations are part of
  Balaban's broader programme. Neither cited source supplies this exact
  common gauge-composite hierarchy with the later OPE/stress requirements.

  <strong|Classification.> STANDARD compatibility/diagonal organization 35%,
  ADAPTED-NLSM source machinery 25%, candidate-NEW all-label extension 40%;
  medium confidence. The enlarged gauge-composite spaces, growing
  derivative/degree requirements and simultaneous OPE/stress comparisons are
  substantive. The order \Pchoose one trajectory, then all spin moments\Q
  already occurs in N3 Theorem 1.2, p.6, and Ÿ9.1, pp.75\U76; that quantifier
  order alone is not new. This is a leading location for new constructive
  work whose detailed priority still needs a wider literature comparison.

  MRS93 p.329 explicitly excludes renormalized physical composite-operator
  construction, while its ŸVIII pp.377\U378 does treat arbitrary elementary
  field insertions. BY90 p.359 Eq.(1.13) already controls all variational
  derivative orders in an analytic norm. The surviving candidate is therefore
  the simultaneous gauge-composite quotient/rank/stress/OPE construction, not
  \Pall insertions,\Q \Pall derivatives,\Q or one common trajectory in
  isolation.

  <subsection|23. Iteration and selection of one construction with uniform
  bounds \V <hlink|III.14|outline/units/III-14.tm>><label|23-iteration-and-selection-of-one-construction-with-uniform-bounds--iii14>

  <strong|Function and size.> 14,239 lines. Local estimates, terminal
