  reinforce the distinction between standard derivative/source operations and
  the Lean-specific quantitative bounds for all gauge composites.

  <subsection|25. <hlink|IV.1|outline/units/IV-1.tm> \V Infinite-volume
  extraction and Euclidean hierarchy><label|25-iv1--infinite-volume-extraction-and-euclidean-hierarchy>

  <strong|Size:> 7,336 lines in spans of declarations used by the main
  theorem.

  <strong|What it does.> It turns volume-uniform observable bounds into a
  common limit table, retains fixed-volume mesh limits, extracts volumes, and
  passes separated smeared correlations to that table. The distinction
  between generic point limits and integrated limits is represented
  explicitly.

  <strong|Lean evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Extraction/Lipschitz.lean:156\U160>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Extraction/Lipschitz.lean#L156-L160>
  defines <hlink|<verbatim|RawLimits>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Extraction/Lipschitz.lean#L156-L160>
  with continuity, mesh, volume and integrated clauses.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Extraction/Held.lean:135\U137>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Extraction/Held.lean#L135-L137>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|crops>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Extraction/Held.lean#L135-L137>,
  obtains a common extraction from the projected chessboard estimates.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Extraction/Limits.lean:57\U66>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Extraction/Limits.lean#L57-L66>
  distinguishes uniform fixed-period mesh convergence from volume convergence
  on compact generic configurations. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Extraction/Integrate.lean:115\U153>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Extraction/Integrate.lean#L115-L153>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|ordinary>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Extraction/Integrate.lean#L115-L153>,
  applies dominated convergence after a common compact-support bound. The
  proof explicitly invokes dominated convergence at
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Extraction/Integrate.lean:152>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Extraction/Integrate.lean#L152-L152>.

  <strong|Closest antecedents.> N3 Proposition 10.2, pp.84\U86, first
  establishes fixed-torus source convergence, then approximates continuous
  tests and exchanges the cutoff and volume limits using equation (10.8). The
  abstract compactness, diagonal extraction and dominated-convergence steps
  are STANDARD. The upstream use of exact renormalized local sources to
  obtain a common limiting hierarchy is the same general method as N3
  ŸŸ9\U10. The exact infinite-volume mechanism differs: N3 uses uniform
  exponentially small finite-volume errors from a transfer-gap construction;
  the Yang\UMills public target permits subsequences.

  <strong|Classification:> STANDARD + ADAPTED-NLSM at the
  observable-construction interface; medium confidence on immediate lineage,
  high on the difference in conclusions. A claim that infinite volume itself
  is a direct transplant of N3 would be too strong.

  <strong|Rough mechanism split:> STANDARD 75%, NLSM source-construction
  affinity 15%, specialized extension 10%.

  <subsection|26. <hlink|V.1|outline/units/V-1.tm> \V Phase
  decomposition><label|26-v1--phase-decomposition>

  <strong|Size:> 5,718 lines in spans of declarations used by the main
  theorem.

  <strong|What it does.> Reflection positivity gives a Hilbert space and
  positive-time contraction semigroup. Compressed whole-word observables on
  its zero-energy space generate an algebra; proving that algebra commutative
  permits a compact character-space mixture of phases.

  <strong|Lean evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/OS/Dynamics.lean:63\U107>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/OS/Dynamics.lean#L63-L107>
  constructs the semigroup and transverse unitary action on the reflection
  Hilbert space. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Ground/Permute.lean:73\U93>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Ground/Permute.lean#L73-L93>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|exchanges>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Ground/Permute.lean#L73-L93>,
  interchanges the middle two entries in a four-entry train by using two
  coordinate directions and a signed rotation.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Ground/Permute.lean:95\U136>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Ground/Permute.lean#L95-L136>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|gens>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Ground/Permute.lean#L95-L136>,
  turns that identity into commutation of the compressed operators.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Ground/Permute.lean:149\U161>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Ground/Permute.lean#L149-L161>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|adjunction>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Ground/Permute.lean#L149-L161>,
  extends commutation to their closed star algebra.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Phase/Mixture.lean:65\U86>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Phase/Mixture.lean#L65-L86>
  specifies the compact character space, probability measure, full support
  and representation of the ground-state functional.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Phase/Mixture.lean:113\U117>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Phase/Mixture.lean#L113-L117>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|barycenter>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Phase/Mixture.lean#L113-L117>,
  gives the correlation mixture identity.

  <strong|Closest antecedents.> The first step is OS73 Ÿ4(a\Ub), p.90:
  positive form, quotient/completion, self-adjoint contraction semigroup.
  Gelfand representation and a positive functional represented by a measure
  are STANDARD functional analysis; they are not distinctively Balaban or
  NLSM. The inspected N3 proof does not make this phase decomposition: Ÿ11.2,
  p.91, obtains vacuum uniqueness from already-established exponential
  clustering. Its target has a single canonical periodic state and a positive
  gap (Theorems 1.1\U1.2).

  <strong|Classification:> STANDARD core with candidate-NEW implementation of
  the train/axis exchange and phase-uniform machinery; medium confidence for
  novelty relative to these sources, low for priority across the literature.
  There is no evidence here that the phase-algebra argument is inherited from
  N3. Before a strong novelty claim, compare the extremal-state and
  asymptotic-abelian constructions in the older Euclidean/algebraic-QFT
  literature.

  <strong|Rough mechanism split:> STANDARD 70%, candidate-NEW phase-uniform
  organization 30%.

  <subsection|27. <hlink|V.2|outline/units/V-2.tm> \V
  Reconstruction><label|27-v2--reconstruction>

  <strong|Size:> 6,419 lines in spans of declarations used by the main
  theorem.

  <strong|What it does.> It builds analytic continuations and boundary
  distributions from Euclidean data, then realizes fields on a Hilbert space
  with locality, spectrum and covariance properties. The reconstruction
  theorems have explicit hypotheses; the assembly discharges them for the
  chosen hierarchy.

  <strong|Lean evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Hilbert/Kolmogorov.lean:67\U92>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Hilbert/Kolmogorov.lean#L67-L92>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|host>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Hilbert/Kolmogorov.lean#L67-L92>,
  completes a positive kernel and proves density of the generating vectors.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Work/C01.lean:99\U117>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C01.lean#L99-L117>
  derives a common controlled field-vector family from slab bounds.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Integrated.lean:182\U212>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Integrated.lean#L182-L212>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|reconstructionArcs>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Integrated.lean#L182-L212>,
  assembles A16 and C01\UC29. <hlink|<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean:135\U143>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean#L135-L143>
  makes analytic continuation, its Euclidean root, and distributional
  boundary convergence explicit. <hlink|<verbatim|PublicTheory.lean:270\U319>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/PublicTheory.lean#L270-L319>
  lists the resulting spectrum, unique-vacuum, domain, covariance and
  locality clauses.

  <strong|Closest antecedents.> OS73 Ÿ4 p.90 gives the null quotient, Hilbert
  completion, contraction semigroup and boundary-value route. Its originally
  announced sufficiency theorem cannot be cited without the correction: OS75
  p.282 identifies OS73 Lemma 8.8 as false. OS75 ŸIII p.285 uses a stronger
  distribution topology; ŸIV.1 p.287 Eq.(4.1) gives the useful sufficient
  linear-growth condition, with one fixed seminorm slope and factorial bounds
  in field order. Its ŸIV.2 pp.288\U289 separates analytic continuation from
  the stronger estimates needed for tempered boundary values. N3 Proposition
  11.3, pp.90\U91, applies the corrected theorem, checking a factorial/linear
  growth estimate. That NLSM application is not the origin of reconstruction:
  it is another use of the same older theorem.

  <strong|Classification:> predominantly STANDARD/ADAPTED-OTHER
  (Osterwalder\USchrader), high confidence. The technical adaptation is to a
  graded gauge-invariant field algebra with all finite-degree labels and to
  phase-dependent tables, rather than a three-component scalar spin field.
  The cited passages do not establish equivalence of the graded Lean
  growth/topology hypotheses with OS75 Eq.(4.1).
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Work/C01.lean:99\U117>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C01.lean#L99-L117>
  supplies a controlled vector family, not that displayed joint Schwartz
  bound by itself. The remaining issue is a separate theorem/domain
  comparison, not lack of access. A direct reconstruction may prove boundary
  estimates by another route; no gap follows from this unresolved
  correspondence.

  <strong|Rough mechanism split:> STANDARD/ADAPTED-OTHER 95%, specialized
  implementation 5%.

  <subsection|28. <hlink|V.3|outline/units/V-3.tm> \V Reconstruction for
  every phase><label|28-v3--reconstruction-for-every-phase>

  <strong|Size:> 4,063 lines in spans of declarations used by the main
  theorem.

  <strong|What it does.> The same field labels, stress data and rotation
  action are carried into each character phase. Uniform estimates establish
  the zero-energy projection and decay after subtracting it, so vacuum
  uniqueness is obtained phase by phase without asserting a mass gap.

  <strong|Lean evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/EveryPhase/Pack.lean:117\U132>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/EveryPhase/Pack.lean#L117-L132>
  defines an <hlink|<verbatim|Almanac>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/EveryPhase/Pack.lean#L117-L132>
  containing every chamber, field theory, label identification and attachment
  to the common data. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/EveryPhase/Extension.lean:75\U80>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/EveryPhase/Extension.lean#L75-L80>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|all_characters>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/EveryPhase/Extension.lean#L75-L80>,
  assembles the completed family. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/EveryPhase/Vacuum.lean:115\U144>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/EveryPhase/Vacuum.lean#L115-L144>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|all_plant>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/EveryPhase/Vacuum.lean#L115-L144>,
  identifies the zero-energy spectral projection with the vacuum rank-one
  operator on a dense family and extends it by continuity.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/EveryPhase/Decay.lean:173\U192>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/EveryPhase/Decay.lean#L173-L192>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|inverse_square>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/EveryPhase/Decay.lean#L173-L192>,
  bounds the residual semigroup matrix element by <math|M*s<rsup|-2>>,
  uniformly in phase. The proof uses a low-energy spectral-mass bound
  <math|\<nu\><around|(|<around|(|0,x|]>|)>\<leqslant\>D*x<rsup|2>> at
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/EveryPhase/Decay.lean:182\U186>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/EveryPhase/Decay.lean#L182-L186>.
  That input is stated explicitly as <hlink|<verbatim|YM/EveryPhase/Light.lean:221\U232>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/EveryPhase/Light.lean#L221-L232>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|light_bound>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/EveryPhase/Light.lean#L221-L232>;
  its proof uses locality at large spatial separation and the spectral
  annulus estimate.

  <strong|Closest antecedents.> N3 Proposition 11.4, pp.91\U93, also uses
  positive spectral measures and a dense set of field-generated vectors. But
  there the semigroup matrix elements are exponentially bounded and hence a
  whole interval of low energies is excluded. Here the low-energy mass
  estimate implies polynomial decay after subtraction of the full zero-energy
  projection, and does not exclude arbitrarily small positive energies.

  <strong|Classification:> STANDARD spectral reasoning +
  candidate-NEW/ADAPTED-OTHER phase-uniform organization; medium confidence.
  This is a comparison at the level of a general method with N3, with a
  materially different estimate and endpoint. It supplies counterevidence to
  the idea that the Yang\UMills continuum construction is simply the NLSM
  mass-gap proof with another target manifold.

  <strong|Rough mechanism split:> STANDARD spectral tools 65%, candidate-NEW
  phase-uniform organization 35%.

  OS75 p.283, Remark 5, already permits extension to denumerably many spinor
  fields; going beyond one scalar is not a new reconstruction principle. OS73
  p.89 also distinguishes exponential clustering and a mass gap. The
  potential contribution here is the particular phase-uniform input
  estimates, notably <hlink|<verbatim|YM/EveryPhase/Light.lean:221\U232>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/EveryPhase/Light.lean#L221-L232>,
  not reconstruction applied to several fields or a family by itself.
  Confidence in that specific corpus-relative extension remains medium.

  <subsection|29. <hlink|VI.1|outline/units/VI-1.tm> (a) \V Ward identities
  and full rotation covariance><label|29-vi1-a--ward-identities-and-full-rotation-covariance>

  <strong|Size:> within <hlink|VI.1|outline/units/VI-1.tm>'s 7,689 lines in
  spans of declarations used by the main theorem.

  <strong|What it does.> Local stress-shell identities produce translations
  and rotation generators on finite-degree fields. The proof upgrades
  collinear identities to all separated configurations and supplies a genuine
  compact rotation action compatible with the field filtration.

  <strong|Lean evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Euclid/Stress.lean:88\U97>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Euclid/Stress.lean#L88-L97>
  defines stress laws on separated configurations; it explicitly says contact
  terms are not tested. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Route.lean:114\U123>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Route.lean#L114-L123>
  proves the collinear angular sum and a line-sum identity.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Ward/Walk.lean:15\U26>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Ward/Walk.lean#L15-L26>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Ward/Walk.lean:60\U84>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Ward/Walk.lean#L60-L84>
  supplies analytic continuation along segments and walks.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Ward/Axis.lean:86\U107>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Ward/Axis.lean#L86-L107>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Ward/Axis.lean:130\U139>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Ward/Axis.lean#L130-L139>
  connects arranged configurations to collinear ones and then completes the
  argument by density and continuity. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SharpGram/Weak/Compatible.lean:140\U159>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SharpGram/Weak/Compatible.lean#L140-L159>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|tune_grades>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SharpGram/Weak/Compatible.lean#L140-L159>,
  constructs changes of coordinates compatible across all degrees up to a
  fixed horizon and preserves units/reality/signed symmetries.

  <strong|Closest antecedents.> Local current insertions generating
  infinitesimal symmetry are STANDARD Ward/Noether reasoning. N3
  ŸŸ10.2\U10.3, pp.84\U87, are an especially useful <em|negative> comparator:
  rotations there follow from comparing tilted regulators and a dense cyclic
  subgroup, not from constructing a local spacetime stress tensor. A direct
  NLSM attribution for the Lean rotation argument is unsupported by this
  source pair.

  <strong|Classification:> STANDARD Ward principle + candidate-NEW
  implementation beyond the inspected NLSM and Balaban UV outputs; medium
  confidence. Claiming originality across the literature of the
  analytic-vector and finite-dimensional representation steps would be
  unjustified; those are established tools.

  <strong|Rough mechanism split:> STANDARD Ward/representation tools 45%,
  candidate-NEW constructive implementation 55%.

  <subsection|30. <hlink|VI.1|outline/units/VI-1.tm> (b) \V Exterior flux at
  infinity><label|30-vi1-b--exterior-flux-at-infinity>

  <strong|Size:> crosses units; the exterior-flux proof occupies
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean:1\U115>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean#L1-L115>
  (115 lines) and <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Route.lean:1\U124>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Route.lean#L1-L124>
  (124 lines), with upstream estimates for coordinate directions.

  <strong|What it does.> It proves that the exterior terms left by large
  rotation cutoffs vanish, allowing a local shell identity to become a global
  infinitesimal rotation identity without an assumed exponential clustering
  estimate.

  <strong|Lean evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean:49\U77>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean#L49-L77>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|pick_price>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean#L49-L77>,
  constructs reflection Hilbert chambers in every coordinate direction,
  splits signs, uses <hlink|<verbatim|exterior_size>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean#L67-L70>,
  and obtains a decay bound with exponent <math|-1> for the actual stress
  integrand. The realizations in all coordinate directions and the upstream
  spectral estimate appear at <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean:54>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean#L54-L54>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean:67\U70>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean#L67-L70>.
  The input <hlink|<verbatim|S.Hierarchy>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Euclid/Words.lean#L140-L148>
  in <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Euclid/Words.lean:140\U148>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Euclid/Words.lean#L140-L148>
  contains positivity, translation invariance, smoothness and bounds, with no
  clustering or mass-gap clause. At <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean:73\U77>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean#L73-L77>,
  a factor with budget 100 combines with budget \<minus\>101 to leave budget
  \<minus\>1. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean:105\U114>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean#L105-L114>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|blows>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean#L105-L114>,
  transfers this to the concrete correlation function for collinear
  insertions. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Route.lean:49\U67>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Route.lean#L49-L67>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|drained>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Route.lean#L49-L67>
  and <hlink|<verbatim|axisTurn>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Route.lean#L49-L67>,
  sum the exterior terms and conclude that a constant with a decaying budget
  is zero.

  <strong|Closest antecedent.> N3 Ÿ11.1\U11.2, pp.89\U93, uses exponential
  clustering and positive-energy spectral reconstruction, but does not
  contain this all-axis exterior-flux device. General reflection positivity
  and spectral Cauchy\USchwarz are standard; the particular cutoff/sign
  construction is a candidate distinct contribution.

  <strong|Classification:> candidate-NEW, medium confidence within these
  sources, low on exhaustive priority. The source reading covers the proof
  through its use of the exterior spectral estimate, but not every upstream
  moment lemma for coordinate directions. This supports the mechanism
  comparison, not a complete verification of the flux estimate.

  <strong|Rough mechanism split:> STANDARD spectral inequalities 35%,
  candidate-NEW exterior-flux organization 65%.

  <subsection|31. <hlink|VI.2|outline/units/VI-2.tm> \V Maxwell reference
  theory and stress normalization><label|31-vi2--maxwell-reference-theory-and-stress-normalization>

  <strong|Size:> 14,170 lines in spans of declarations used by the main
  theorem.

  <strong|What it does.> It supplies a free Maxwell hierarchy on the same
  graded label format, its conserved stress tensor and its shell Ward action.
  The free reference fixes the interacting stress and rotation structures
  through short-distance comparison.

  <strong|Lean evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/MAXWELL/System.lean:106\U114>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/MAXWELL/System.lean#L106-L114>
  proves the hierarchy; <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/MAXWELL/System.lean:153\U170>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/MAXWELL/System.lean#L153-L170>
  defines the rotation action; <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/MAXWELL/System.lean:172\U179>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/MAXWELL/System.lean#L172-L179>
  defines and proves conservation of the free stress.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/MAXWELL/Current.lean:184\U187>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/MAXWELL/Current.lean#L184-L187>
  defines the shell flux as the integral against the cutoff gradient.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/MAXWELL/Force.lean:131\U186>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/MAXWELL/Force.lean#L131-L186>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|jet_force>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/MAXWELL/Force.lean#L131-L186>,
  extends the point-source flux identity to arbitrary jet probes by linearity
  and differentiation. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SharpGram/Weak/Compatible.lean:140\U159>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SharpGram/Weak/Compatible.lean#L140-L159>
  is a direct anchor for the coherent interacting/free label comparison used
  downstream.

  <strong|Closest antecedents.> Wick contractions and the Maxwell
  stress/Noether identity are textbook free-field calculations. N3 Ÿ11.3,
  pp.93\U94, compares renormalized sources to a terminal spin law; it does
  not construct this Maxwell jet algebra or use it to fix spacetime stress.
  The two share the general tactic of identifying limiting operators by a
  controlled comparison model, not the same reference theory or normalization
  mechanism.

  <strong|Classification:> mostly STANDARD free-field mathematics, with
  candidate-NEW assembly/normalization in this nonperturbative construction;
  high confidence for the standard part, medium for the adaptation. The
  number of Lean lines devoted to Wick jets should not be interpreted as a
  comparable number of new physics ideas.

  <strong|Rough mechanism split:> STANDARD Maxwell mathematics 80%,
  specialized normalization 20%.

  <subsection|32. <hlink|VII.1|outline/units/VII-1.tm> \V Short-distance
  freeness and finite detector systems><label|32-vii1--short-distance-freeness-and-finite-detector-systems>

  <strong|Size:> 8,004 lines in spans of declarations used by the main
  theorem.

  <strong|What it does.> It identifies the leading short-distance
  correlations with Maxwell Wick correlations and chooses compatible
  finite-degree coordinates for the interacting field spaces. Gaussian
  convergence is used to distinguish labels and fix their symmetries, not
  merely to approximate the partition function.

  <strong|Lean evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Pert/Rig/Linked.lean:59\U69>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Pert/Rig/Linked.lean#L59-L69>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|zerothProfile>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Pert/Rig/Linked.lean#L59-L69>,
  identifies the zeroth perturbative coefficient with the smeared free Wick
  moment. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Pert/Warp/Approach.lean:19\U32>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Pert/Warp/Approach.lean#L19-L32>
  proves the moment limit by recursively pairing contractions.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Pert/Warp/Horizons.lean:65\U68>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Pert/Warp/Horizons.lean#L65-L68>
  passes to polynomial rows. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SharpGram/Weak/Compatible.lean:140\U159>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SharpGram/Weak/Compatible.lean#L140-L159>
  gives coherent degree-wise changes of basis.

  <strong|Closest antecedents.> N3 Ÿ5.3, pp.42\U45, uses ambient Gaussian
  completion for an exact RG step; its Ÿ11.3 compares source moments to a
  terminal spin law. Those are related Gaussian/comparison methods, but
  neither cited passage establishes the Maxwell composite-field
  short-distance statement. Asymptotic freedom and Gaussian leading
  short-distance behavior are older perturbative expectations: Gross\UWilczek
  1973 and Politzer 1973, specified below.

  <strong|Classification:> STANDARD Wick/perturbative principle +
  ADAPTED-OTHER constructive implementation and candidate-NEW coherent
  detector use; medium confidence. A comparison of
  <hlink|<verbatim|zerothProfile>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Pert/Rig/Linked.lean#L59-L69>
  alone is insufficient to certify the full interacting asymptotic statement;
  the remainder estimates enter from the ultraviolet units.

  <strong|Rough mechanism split:> STANDARD/ADAPTED-OTHER 60%, candidate-NEW
  coherent detector use 40%.

  <subsection|33. <hlink|VII.2|outline/units/VII-2.tm> \V Trace anomaly and
  the all-radii asymptotic><label|33-vii2--trace-anomaly-and-the-all-radii-asymptotic>

  <strong|Size:> 20,645 lines in spans of declarations used by the main
  theorem, including some nonfree exclusions.

  <strong|What it does.> The centered trace is tied to the physical stress
  tensor and its short-distance two-point behavior is normalized. An
  asymptotic first obtained along admissible scaling systems is promoted to
  every small positive radius.

  <strong|Lean evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/TraceExtension/WilsonTrace.lean:48\U49>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/WilsonTrace.lean#L48-L49>
  defines the centered trace in the selected phase.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Nonperturbative/HeadlineSpec.lean:84\U95>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Nonperturbative/HeadlineSpec.lean#L84-L95>
  explicitly identifies its field operator with stress trace minus its vacuum
  expectation and states the short-distance law.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/TraceExtension/Verse/Day.lean:41>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/Verse/Day.lean#L41-L41>
  defines the intermediate amplitude <math|12*<around|(|d<rsub|2><rsup|2>/b<rsub|0><rsup|2>|)>*dim
  \<frak-g\>/\<pi\><rsup|4>>. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/TraceExtension/Verse/Cape.lean:53\U76>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/Verse/Cape.lean#L53-L76>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|border>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/Verse/Cape.lean#L53-L76>,
  uses monotonicity of the positive spectral Laplace transform to compare
  nearby radii. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/TraceExtension/Verse/Cape.lean:77\U88>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/Verse/Cape.lean#L77-L88>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|outside>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/Verse/Cape.lean#L77-L88>,
  argues by a bad sequence of radii and the ability to fit a subsequence to a
  scaling system. The public numerical conclusion is
  <hlink|<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean:172\U174>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean#L172-L174>:
  <math|3*dim \<frak-g\>/\<pi\><rsup|4>> after multiplication by
  <math|r<rsup|8>*log<rsup|2><around|(|1/r|)>>.

  <strong|Closest antecedents.> CDJ77 ŸŸII\UIV pp.439\U445 gives the
  renormalized normal-product/operator-mixing trace law, formulated in terms
  of beta functions and anomalous dimensions beyond a one-loop slogan. Its
  full Eq.(3.19), p.443, retains gauge-fixing, ghost and equation-of-motion
  operators; the simpler full-trace Eq.(3.21) is stated for on-shell
  nonzero-momentum matrix elements. Section IV extends the operator relation
  to arbitrary momentum. The beta-function trace mechanism is therefore a
  detailed older antecedent. It does not by itself yield a constructed
  positive Euclidean hierarchy or the all-radii two-point limit. Identifying
  coefficients requires fixing the coupling inside the curvature, stress sign
  and normal-product convention; no off-shell equality or numerical
  convention match is inferred merely from the familiar formula. N3 ŸŸ11.3
  and 15.2\U15.3 concern a fourth cumulant and canonical normalization, not a
  four-dimensional stress-trace ultraviolet law. Promoting controlled
  subsequences to a full limit is a standard logical argument; the hard
  construction-specific input is the uniformity allowing all prescribed
  radius sequences.

  <strong|Classification:> old trace-anomaly principle + candidate-NEW
  constructive realization and all-radii control; medium confidence. The
  correspondence is high-confidence for the older trace mechanism and the
  difference in stated endpoint, medium for the specific constructive novelty
  candidate. The one-loop coefficient and its matching to the full
  gauge-fixed determinant remain unaudited in full. Nor should the
  cancellation in the final amplitude be treated as a numerical cross-check
  of that coefficient.

  <strong|Rough mechanism split:> STANDARD/ADAPTED-OTHER anomaly and limit
  principles 40%, candidate-NEW constructive realization 60%.

  <subsection|34. <hlink|VII.3|outline/units/VII-3.tm> \V
  Nonfreeness><label|34-vii3--nonfreeness>

  <strong|Size:> 3,303 lines in spans of declarations used by the main
  theorem for free detection/positivity; the terminal exclusion proof is
  largely included in <hlink|VII.2|outline/units/VII-2.tm>.

  <strong|What it does.> The free reference has a nondegenerate reflected
  Gram form, and the trace singularity rules out finite combinations of
  ordinary free mass-shell Wick contractions. The public claim concerns a
  specified class of comparison profiles.

  <strong|Lean evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean:151\U166>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean#L151-L166>
  defines the mass-shell mode and finite Wick-profile class.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/TraceExtension/Wick/Log.lean:96\U108>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/Wick/Log.lean#L96-L108>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|no_powerlog>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/Wick/Log.lean#L96-L108>,
  proves that a profile with the specified finite power/log expansion cannot
  have the positive inverse-log asymptotic.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/TraceExtension/Wick/Refute.lean:75\U80>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/Wick/Refute.lean#L75-L80>
  applies this to the Euclidean trace. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/TraceExtension/Wick/Refute.lean:86\U105>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/Wick/Refute.lean#L86-L105>
  transfers the exclusion to smeared polynomial-field descriptions. The
  module's precise limitation is at <hlink|<verbatim|YM/TraceExtension/Wick/Refute.lean:4\U8>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/Wick/Refute.lean#L4-L8>:
  it makes no claim against a continuous-mass generalized free field.

  <strong|Closest antecedent and difference.> N3 Proposition 11.5, pp.93\U94,
  proves non-Gaussian behavior by a nonzero separated connected four-point
  function. It computes the leading cumulant from the uniform sphere moments
  <math|\<bbb-E\><around|[|<around|(|U<rsup|1>|)><rsup|2>|]>=1/3>,
  <math|\<bbb-E\><around|[|<around|(|U<rsup|1>|)><rsup|4>|]>=1/5>. That is a
  different witness from the trace inverse logarithm; \Pnontriviality from a
  controlled nonfree observable\Q is only the shared general method.

  <strong|Classification:> candidate-NEW choice and rigorous realization of a
  nonfree witness; STANDARD asymptotic algebra once the trace law is known.
  High confidence on difference from N3; medium/low confidence on broad
  novelty because no exhaustive search for older trace-based nontriviality
  arguments was made.

  <strong|Rough mechanism split:> STANDARD asymptotic exclusion 50%,
  candidate-NEW constructive witness 50%.

  <subsection|35. <hlink|VII.4|outline/units/VII-4.tm> (a) \V Separated
  OPE><label|35-vii4-a--separated-ope>

  <strong|Size:> part of 10,193 lines in spans of declarations used by the
  main theorem.

  <strong|What it does.> The product of clustered insertions is expanded in
  finite-degree local fields with a remainder bounded by its next degree,
  with arbitrary small power slack. One coefficient system works for the
  mixture and every character phase, independently of the spectator word and
  truncation horizon. For each fixed spectator word the bounds are uniform in
  phase and allowed spectator positions; the eventual radius threshold may
  depend on that word, as quantified in <hlink|<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean:74\U76>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean#L74-L76>.

  <strong|Lean evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/OPE/Orbits/Kernel.lean:4\U25>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/OPE/Orbits/Kernel.lean#L4-L25>
  states the intended exact scope and excludes convergence as the horizon
  tends to infinity. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/OPE/Orbits/Kernel.lean:62\U64>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/OPE/Orbits/Kernel.lean#L62-L64>
  gives the coefficient and algebra-error bounds.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/OPE/Orbits/Kernel.lean:52\U58>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/OPE/Orbits/Kernel.lean#L52-L58>
  passes the same estimates to probability mixtures.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/OPE/Orbits/Kernel.lean:73\U77>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/OPE/Orbits/Kernel.lean#L73-L77>
  constructs one reader before demands by selecting a prescription once.
  <hlink|<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean:70\U80>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean#L70-L80>
  gives the public coefficient/remainder inequalities, with the quantifiers
  for spectators and states visible.

  <strong|Closest antecedents.> W69 ŸII pp.1500\U1501 Eq.(2.2) already
  proposes a weak OPE between fixed states, with finitely many terms at each
  finite order, powers and logarithms, elementary or composite fields, and
  extensions to multiple products. Its coefficient functions do not depend on
  the external states. Thus finite-order organization, composites and common
  coefficients are older ideas, not candidate innovations of this
  construction. Zimmermann, <em|Normal products and the short distance
  expansion in the perturbation theory of renormalizable interactions>
  (1973), pp.570\U601, publisher abstract, proves Wilson's expansion in
  perturbation theory for two scalar fields. These are substantial conceptual
  antecedents; neither supplies the displayed nonperturbative Yang\UMills
  hierarchy and its quantitative power-with-slack bounds, uniform over
  allowed positions and phases for each fixed spectator word. Zimmermann's
  full paper remains unavailable; only its abstract is used. Wilson's common
  coefficients alone are not evidence of novelty in the Lean detector
  prescription. The inspected N3 ŸŸ9\U11 renormalize the spin sources and
  their moments, with no corresponding all-composite, all-phase OPE statement
  in those sections or its main theorem.

  <strong|Classification:> ADAPTED-OTHER for the finite-order weak/composite
  OPE framework, high confidence; candidate-NEW only for the constructed
  hierarchy's quantitative estimates and compatibility, medium confidence
  relative to the corpus and low for worldwide novelty. Do not call the
  existence of an OPE itself a new idea. Do not claim perturbative
  coefficient matching, convergence in truncation order, or uniqueness of the
  prescription.

  <strong|Rough mechanism split:> ADAPTED-OTHER OPE principle and standard
  expansion tools 60%, candidate-NEW uniform implementation 40%.

  <subsection|36. <hlink|VII.4|outline/units/VII-4.tm> (b) \V Contact
  extensions><label|36-vii4-b--contact-extensions>

  <strong|Size:> within <hlink|VII.4|outline/units/VII-4.tm>; about 3,400
  lines for contact extensions and about 290 for multiple-contact extensions.

  <strong|What it does.> The separated correlation functions are extended to
  distributions on collision diagonals using normal Taylor subtractions and
  sums over collapse scales and forests. The resulting extension agrees with
  the original hierarchy away from coincidences.

  <strong|Lean evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Contacts/Normal/Subtractions.lean:54\U75>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Contacts/Normal/Subtractions.lean#L54-L75>
  defines the normal Taylor polynomial, cutoff, and <math|1-T> remainder on
  test functions. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Contacts/Normal/Subtractions.lean:76\U83>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Contacts/Normal/Subtractions.lean#L76-L83>
  proves commutation of subtractions in orthogonal normal directions.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Contacts/Solstice/Result.lean:23\U31>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Contacts/Solstice/Result.lean#L23-L31>
  records the shell-sum identities and summability over admissible forests.
  <hlink|<verbatim|ContactSpec.lean:10\U14>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ContactSpec.lean#L10-L14>
  is exactly an extension-and-agreement statement for distributions on the
  full configuration space. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Contacts/Solstice/Result.lean:3\U6>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Contacts/Solstice/Result.lean#L3-L6>
  explicitly distinguishes this from reflection positivity on overlapping
  supports, contact Ward identities, and real-time time-ordered products.

  <strong|Closest antecedent.> BF00 Ÿ5.2, Theorems 5.2\U5.3, preprint
  pp.23\U25, gives the same core distribution-extension device. Equation (36)
  sums dyadic shells; equation (38) projects a test onto its Taylor-vanishing
  part; equation (39) defines an extension after subtraction. BF00 Ÿ6 then
  treats submanifolds and transverse scaling, making the normal-variable
  comparison more than a superficial resemblance.

  <strong|Classification:> ADAPTED-OTHER/STANDARD for extension by Taylor
  subtraction, high confidence; candidate-NEW input bounds and organization
  for this hierarchy, medium confidence. This is not persuasive evidence of
  NLSM ancestry: the pertinent older distribution-renormalization literature
  is a closer match. Existence of an extension is weaker than identification
  of a unique physical product at coincidence.

  <strong|Rough mechanism split:> STANDARD/ADAPTED-OTHER extension mechanism
  80%, specialized input/organization 20%.

  OS73 Ÿ3 p.89, Remark 1, already distinguishes separated Euclidean data from
  coincidence values: reconstruction does not determine the latter. This
  conceptual distinction, read with OS75's corrected setting, reinforces the
  scope limit; it is not a source for the whole forest-subtraction proof.
  W69's discussion of composite and time-ordered products likewise does not
  identify a unique physical contact extension here.

  <subsection|37. <hlink|VII.5|outline/units/VII-5.tm> \V Leading-log
  coupling asymptotics><label|37-vii5--leading-log-coupling-asymptotics>

  <strong|Size:> 232 lines in spans of declarations used by the main theorem,
  only a small fraction of which is the coupling theorem itself.

  <strong|What it does.> It converts previously established running-coupling
  control into the asymptotic for the inverse square root of the action
  coefficient. It does not recompute the one-loop constant in this unit.

  <strong|Lean evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Pert/Asymptotics/RawAxis.lean:13\U16>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Pert/Asymptotics/RawAxis.lean#L13-L16>
  defines <math|b<rsub|0>=11*C<rsub|A>/<around|(|48*\<pi\><rsup|2>|)>> and
  states the ratio estimate. The proof at
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Pert/Asymptotics/RawAxis.lean:17\U30>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Pert/Asymptotics/RawAxis.lean#L17-L30>
  reads the <hlink|<verbatim|Makes.wilson.running>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Pert/Asymptotics/RawAxis.lean#L17-L30>
  estimate and removes the fixed additive offset. At
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Pert/Asymptotics/RawAxis.lean:35\U48>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Pert/Asymptotics/RawAxis.lean#L35-L48>
  it takes inverse square roots to get the leading-log limit. The module says
  at <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Pert/Asymptotics/RawAxis.lean:3\U4>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Pert/Asymptotics/RawAxis.lean#L3-L4>
  that this proxy is not identified with a physical X-scheme coupling beyond
  the proved comparison.

  <strong|Closest antecedents.> Gross\UWilczek, PRL 30 (1973), 1343\U1346,
  and Politzer, PRL 30 (1973), 1346\U1349, are the older asymptotic-freedom
  source. N3 equation (1.4), p.6, and Ÿ12.1, p.95, likewise encode a linear
  inverse-coupling trajectory with a model-specific coefficient. The same
  general marginal-flow mechanism is present, but <math|11*C<rsub|A>> is
  Yang\UMills data, not the sigma-model <math|1/<around|(|2*\<pi\>|)>>
  coefficient.

  <strong|Classification:> STANDARD consequence of an earlier nonstandard
  construction, high confidence. The provenance of the coefficient
  calculation belongs to the actual one-loop/matching units, not to this
  short endpoint theorem.

  <strong|Rough mechanism split:> STANDARD endpoint implication about 100%;
  the upstream coefficient and flow estimates are counted in
  <hlink|III.7|outline/units/III-7.tm>.

  <subsection|38. <hlink|VII.6|outline/units/VII-6.tm> \V Flexible scaling
  and arbitrary radius sequences><label|38-vii6--flexible-scaling-and-arbitrary-radius-sequences>

  <strong|Size:> 4,205 lines in spans of declarations used by the main
  theorem.

  <strong|What it does.> It produces valid scaling stations along
  subsequences of an arbitrary given sequence of radii tending to zero. These
  stations retain the finite-degree observable controls needed to derive a
  full real-radius trace asymptotic.

  <strong|Lean evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|YM/TraceExtension/Prom/Zoom/Boats.lean:49\U62>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/Prom/Zoom/Boats.lean#L49-L62>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|boatsSky>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/Prom/Zoom/Boats.lean#L49-L62>,
  fits a sufficiently small prescribed radius to a valid station.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/TraceExtension/Prom/Zoom/Boats.lean:75\U87>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/Prom/Zoom/Boats.lean#L75-L87>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|wakesSky>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/Prom/Zoom/Boats.lean#L75-L87>,
  recursively obtains a strictly monotone subsequence and stations of
  increasing degree. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/TraceExtension/Verse/Cape.lean:77\U88>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/Verse/Cape.lean#L77-L88>
  uses that flexibility to obtain the all-radii conclusion.

  <strong|Closest antecedent.> N3 Ÿ15.2\U15.3, pp.118\U120, removes
  subsequence ambiguity by comparing bounded-offset trajectories and
  canonically normalizing them. The common logical purpose is to upgrade
  control of selected scales to an unrestricted limit, but the mechanisms
  differ: coupling-offset matching versus fitting arbitrary radii to stopping
  stations.

  <strong|Classification:> STANDARD subsequence logic + candidate-NEW
  technical extension of the construction estimates; medium confidence. This
  should not be counted as an independent new RG principle merely because its
  formal implementation duplicates many estimates.

  <strong|Rough mechanism split:> STANDARD subsequence/limit reasoning 70%,
  specialized uniform-scale estimates 30%.

  <subsection|39. <hlink|VIII.1|outline/units/VIII-1.tm> \V Common-witness
  assembly><label|39-viii1--common-witness-assembly>

  <strong|Size:> 12,056 lines in spans of declarations used by the main
  theorem.

  <strong|What it does.> It selects the construction before extraction
  demands, then places the limit, reconstructed phases, OPE, contact
  extension and trace statements on one common hierarchy.

  <strong|Lean evidence.> <hlink|<verbatim|Construction.lean:15\U31>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/Construction.lean#L15-L31>
  quantifies the family first, the extraction order next, and the
  reader/contact/scaling witnesses afterward.
  <hlink|<verbatim|Construction.lean:32\U40>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/Construction.lean#L32-L40>
  assembles the internal theorem into public properties.
  <hlink|<verbatim|Solution.lean:4\U10>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/Solution.lean#L4-L10>
  specializes the fixed penalty and extracts the public volume refinement.
  <hlink|<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean:3\U4>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean#L3-L4>
  emphasizes that all correlation terms are evaluations of one hierarchy.

  <strong|Closest antecedent.> N3 completion of Theorem 1.2, pp.94\U95,
  explicitly requires the same field normalizations for construction, gap,
  and non-Gaussian correlations. Requiring one object to satisfy all
  conclusions is STANDARD mathematical logic and constructive-QFT discipline.
  The Lean implementation makes an unusually large quantity of compatibility
  and choice-order bookkeeping explicit.

  <strong|Classification:> STANDARD assembly/formal infrastructure, high
  confidence; it should not inflate the share attributed to mathematical
  novelty.

  <strong|Rough mechanism split:> STANDARD assembly/formal compatibility 95%,
  specialized organization 5%.

  <section|4. Cross-cutting comparisons><label|4-cross-cutting-comparisons>

  <subsection|4.1 Where the ultraviolet construction
  ends><label|41-where-the-ultraviolet-construction-ends>

  Balaban's cited pure-gauge sequence ends in uniform effective-density
  stability under the small-running-coupling assumption, B-Large-II
  pp.355\U356. The later correlation analysis is not present there. That is a
  limitation of this particular gauge endpoint, not a statement about all his
  work. B-N-Corr and B-Corr explicitly study correlations in other models.

  N2 joins exact weak-coupling blocking to preliminary mixing and a
  rectangular partition-function defect, ŸŸ2\U3,8\U9. The terminal finite-box
  input is transported to fine cutoffs, and repeated positivity/doubling
  gives a full gap. N3 builds the spin field on top of this massive route,
  ŸŸ8\U11, then removes the bounded-offset ambiguity, ŸŸ12\U15. Its continuum
  endpoint is canonical for the specified normalization and state.

  The Lean instead proceeds from a common all-label construction,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SharpGram/Assembly/Flowers.lean:75\U130>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SharpGram/Assembly/Flowers.lean#L75-L130>,
  to fixed-volume full-mesh convergence and volume extraction. It then builds
  phases, reconstructs each phase internally and identifies
  stress/rotation/OPE data on the common hierarchy. The internal
  <hlink|<verbatim|Construction.lean:15\U31>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/Construction.lean#L15-L31>
  result includes contacts and reconstruction of every phase;
  <hlink|<verbatim|Solution.lean:4\U10>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/Solution.lean#L4-L10>
  exports a selected reconstructed theory and the common phase table through
  <hlink|<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean:24\U42>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean#L24-L42>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean:117\U143>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean#L117-L143>.
  The full internal package should not be confused with the exact list of
  conclusions literally exported by <hlink|<verbatim|JointExistence>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean#L186-L188>.
  <hlink|<verbatim|Construction.lean:15\U31>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/Construction.lean#L15-L31>
  makes this order visible in the quantifiers.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/EveryPhase/Decay.lean:173\U192>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/EveryPhase/Decay.lean#L173-L192>
  provides polynomial residual decay, not a positive spectral threshold. Thus
  <strong|the NLSM resemblance is strongest before the infrared endpoint, and
  weakens where the two theories are identified>. The absence of a gap
  assertion is not a proof of gaplessness.

  MRS93 is a substantial direct four-dimensional gauge UV comparator. Its
  pp.325\U327,352\U355 uses regularized axial gauge, local
  background-dependent gauges and boson/ghost propagators, stabilizing
  cutoffs and symmetry-restoring counterterms for SU(2) in the trivial
  topological sector. Section VI pp.368\U374 controls large-field determinant
  dressing; ŸŸVII\UVIII pp.374\U378 treats coupling-flow remainders and
  Slavnov identities.

  Its stated ultraviolet limit consists of gauge-fixed elementary Schwinger
  functions with a fixed infrared cutoff, p.327. That page expressly leaves a
  fully detailed proof unwritten and OS positivity as additional work. Page
  329 explicitly excludes construction there of the renormalized physical
  composite operators; arbitrary elementary insertions in ŸVIII are not the
  same task. These limits are stronger evidence than a search failing to find
  an observable theorem. Compare the common composite/phase/OPE family at
  <hlink|<verbatim|Construction.lean:15\U31>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/Construction.lean#L15-L31>
  and the volume-refinement target at <hlink|<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean:177\U188>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean#L177-L188>.

  Seiler82 Ÿ5a pp.99\U103 distinguishes a scaling-limit programme from its
  realization: the displayed pure YM loop limit is two dimensional, and the
  stability discussion is three dimensional. His external-field matter
  determinants in Ÿ6b pp.118\U121 do not constitute a four-dimensional
  gauge-field construction. Neither older source should be described as
  lacking a continuum discussion; neither supplies the stated Lean endpoint.

  <subsection|4.2 Reflection positivity and chessboard
  estimates><label|42-reflection-positivity-and-chessboard-estimates>

  FILS78 Ÿ4, preprint pp.33\U36, already proves abstract and torus chessboard
  inequalities. Seiler82 Ÿ2a pp.14\U16 supplies direct lattice-gauge
  reflection positivity and the transfer construction, Theorem 2.1. Theorem
  2.2 states a chessboard bound for local matter functions and refers the
  gauge-field extension onward; FILS supplies the general dissemination
  principle. Osterwalder\USeiler 1978 remains unavailable in full: its
  abstract gives the reflection/transfer and strong-coupling setting, not a
  continuum weak-coupling theorem. The Lean's basic dissemination and
  seminorm statement, <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Cells/Certificate.lean:14\U24>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Cells/Certificate.lean#L14-L24>,
  belongs to that older tradition. Its gauge-law proof is consumed directly
  from <verbatim|Slice.reflected_positive>.

  N2 uses positivity for rectangular doubling and a spectral-gap conclusion,
  ŸŸ2,9. N3 uses both kinetic-row exponential moments, Lemma 7.3, p.62, and
  later massive reconstruction. The Lean uses chessboard roots to control
  composite observables and finite differences,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Extraction/Center.lean:21\U40>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Extraction/Center.lean#L21-L40>,
  and spectral positivity to manage phases and exterior flux. The principle
  is shared; the required estimates and endpoint are different. The word
  \Preflection positivity\Q alone provides no evidence that the mass-gap
  argument was transplanted.

  <subsection|4.3 Composite insertions and
  observables><label|43-composite-insertions-and-observables>

  B-RG-I's gauge identities control Taylor coefficients of effective actions,
  ŸŸ4\U5. B-Corr and B-N-Corr show that correlation analysis belongs to the
  wider Balaban programme. It would be wrong to infer an NLSM origin merely
  because the Lean has observables. MRS93 ŸVIII pp.377\U378 treats arbitrary
  elementary gauge-fixed field insertions and Slavnov identities; p.329
  explicitly leaves renormalized physical composite-operator construction
  outside the paper. This is a direct older source-analysis comparison, with
  a sharply different composite endpoint.

  N3 Ÿ9.1\U9.2, pp.75\U80, carries independent spin sources and normalizes
  their linear response through the flow. The Lean shares exact source
  passage and retained supports: <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Source/Insertion.lean:26\U68>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Source/Insertion.lean#L26-L68>
  defines the inserted coefficient and <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Source/Insertion.lean:89\U110>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Source/Insertion.lean#L89-L110>
  requires its exact push along the same unmarked history. These are
  finite-degree/nilpotent coefficient constructions, not literally N3's
  analytic multivariate spin-source neighborhood; their realization is part
  of the common-family construction in <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SharpGram/Assembly/Flowers.lean:75\U130>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SharpGram/Assembly/Flowers.lean#L75-L130>.
  Its observable target is wider: all finite-degree gauge-invariant composite
  labels, equation quotients, derivatives, stress and OPE channels.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Labels/Polynomial.lean:60\U112>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Labels/Polynomial.lean#L60-L112>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/MergeProof/Powder.lean:19\U71>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/MergeProof/Powder.lean#L19-L71>
  and <hlink|<verbatim|YM/OPE/Orbits/Kernel.lean:62\U77>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/OPE/Orbits/Kernel.lean#L62-L77>
  show these added obligations. The source-propagation strategy is shared;
  the common all-label closure and identification are not supplied by the
  cited spin-source sections.

  W69 ŸII pp.1500\U1501 Eq.(2.2) already includes weak finite-order
  expansions, composite fields, multiple products and coefficients
  independent of external states. Z73 remains an abstract-only perturbative
  comparator. The potential contribution is the nonperturbative quantitative
  remainder for this constructed hierarchy, with bounds uniform in phase and
  allowed positions for each fixed word, together with the common
  composite-field identifications. Common coefficients and finite-order
  organization alone are not new ideas. The eventual radius threshold can
  depend on the spectator word; this is not one bound uniform over all such
  words. Likewise collision extension uses old Taylor-subtraction
  mathematics, BF00 Ÿ5.2, Eqs.(36)\U(39). It does not automatically add
  contact Ward identities or physical time-ordered products;
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Contacts/Solstice/Result.lean:3\U6>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Contacts/Solstice/Result.lean#L3-L6>
  explicitly limits the claim.

  <subsection|4.4 Running coupling, tuning and
  normalization><label|44-running-coupling-tuning-and-normalization>

  Balaban isolates the gauge-action marginal direction by symmetry and Taylor
  analysis, B-RG-I p.258 and Ÿ5. N2 preserves a fixed-reference geometric
  partition while shooting the actual coupling, Eq.(5.1), p.38, and Ÿ7. The
  Lean combines these two features, <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/History.lean:63\U85>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/History.lean#L63-L85>
  and <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/Activities.lean:70\U75>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/Activities.lean#L70-L75>.
  Its exact reset must restore the whole density class,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/AnalyticBridge/VERIFIED_RESET_STEP/Evaluation.lean:71\U95>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/AnalyticBridge/VERIFIED_RESET_STEP/Evaluation.lean#L71-L95>.
  That restoration is more closely formulated in N2 Eqs.(5.40)\U(5.42) than
  in the broad phrase \Prenormalize the coupling\Q.

  The physical universal coefficient comes from gauge vector/ghost algebra,
  not the sigma-model beta function. Abbott82 Ÿ3.3, p.48,
  Eqs.(3.3.1)\U(3.3.5), supplies a directly read older calculation of the
  coefficient. The Lean's momentum-shell calculation and bounded matching are
  at <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/STATIONARY/OneLoop/MomentumIntegral.lean:326\U408>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/STATIONARY/OneLoop/MomentumIntegral.lean#L326-L408>
  and <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/STATIONARY/Level/Transition.lean:153\U178>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/STATIONARY/Level/Transition.lean#L153-L178>.
  The latter explicitly transfers the auxiliary coefficient to the stationary
  one; the matrices must not be conflated.

  Finally, the internal coefficient is a coordinate in the action
  representation. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/History.lean:25\U33>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/History.lean#L25-L33>
  and <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Pert/Asymptotics/RawAxis.lean:3\U4>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Pert/Asymptotics/RawAxis.lean#L3-L4>
  explicitly withhold identification with an independently defined physical
  coupling scheme beyond the proved comparison. N3's canonical normalization
  by susceptibility/second-moment length, Theorem 1.1, pp.5\U6, is a
  different statement. The Lean's successful-recursion tuning uniqueness is
  not a claim that every possible regulator gives the same continuum theory.

  GK85 Ÿ9 pp.233\U237, especially Eq.(9.36), supplies an older controlled
  local-marginal extraction with inverse-coupling increments; Ÿ12 p.246 tunes
  the scalar critical mass by nested intervals. BY90 pp.360\U363
  Eqs.(1.17)\U(1.31) extracts constant/quadratic terms and shifts the
  covariance exactly. MRS93 p.330 Eqs.(II.11)\U(II.12) compares tentative and
  exact gauge-coupling schedules; p.376 explains perturbative orders and
  remainder control. These strengthen the older RG attribution without
  identifying the Lean gauge coefficient. GK's positive scalar coupling is
  infrared asymptotically free, BY's quadratic gradient matrix has a
  different role, and neither can be substituted numerically for the gauge
  running coordinate.

  <subsection|4.5 Order of constants and schedule
  growth><label|45-order-of-constants-and-schedule-growth>

  A correct order of choices is common constructive analysis: D-II Theorem
  3.1, preprint p.29, and B-N-II Theorem 1, p.680, are explicit examples. N2
  p.55, Eq.(5.43), chooses leading uniform constants before block size, later
  coefficient caps and logarithmic powers, then a large coupling threshold.
  The Lean similarly distinguishes pre-block choices from later waits,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/History.lean:43\U61>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/History.lean#L43-L61>
  and <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/FLUCTUATIONS/Grants.lean:32\U36>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/FLUCTUATIONS/Grants.lean#L32-L36>.

  What changes is the demand for one sequence serving unboundedly many finite
  orders. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SharpGram/Assembly/Flowers.lean:77\U130>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SharpGram/Assembly/Flowers.lean#L77-L130>
  increases order requests and selects the family once.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/FullVolume/Sluggish.lean:13\U19>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/FullVolume/Sluggish.lean#L13-L19>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/FullVolume/Sluggish.lean:37\U38>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/FullVolume/Sluggish.lean#L37-L38>
  distinguishes eventual mesh statements uniform in all periods from a merely
  fixed-period bound. This quantified extension is meaningful. The existence
  of many parameters, however, is not evidence for many new ideas, nor proof
  that the dependency order has been verified throughout.

  GK85 p.222 explicitly orders the block factor, locality scale, norm
  constants and final small-coupling threshold; its numerical reference
  envelope is already in p.211 Eq.(4.1) and p.221 Eq.(6.2). BY90 Theorems A/B
  pp.360\U363 orders norm choices and controls cumulative regulator losses,
  with an all-derivative analytic norm at p.359 Eq.(1.13). MRS93 p.330 also
  separates tentative from exact running parameters. These direct antecedents
  rule out novelty of reference schedules, safe constant order or
  all-derivative control in isolation. The narrower Lean question is
  compatibility of growing stages with all gauge-composite, rank, stress and
  OPE demands.

  <subsection|4.6 Correlations, full mesh limits and volume
  limits><label|46-correlations-full-mesh-limits-and-volume-limits>

  N3 Proposition 10.2, pp.84\U86, uses its massive estimates to compare tori
  and pass tests to the limit. Its Ÿ15.3, pp.119\U120, removes the real
  bare-coupling ambiguity. The Lean has a full mesh limit at each suitable
  fixed physical volume, <hlink|<verbatim|PublicWilson.lean:83\U90>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/PublicWilson.lean#L83-L90>,
  followed by a subsequential volume limit,
  <hlink|<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean:184\U188>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean#L184-L188>.
  These quantifiers must remain distinct. The theorem does not thereby supply
  arbitrary simultaneous mesh/volume limits or uniqueness across all
  bare-coupling trajectories.

  Dominated convergence and diagonal compactness are standard.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Extraction/Integrate.lean:115\U153>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Extraction/Integrate.lean#L115-L153>
  explicitly uses a dominated-convergence theorem. The substantial
  constructive input is uniform control of the renormalized observables
  before that theorem is applied. A large number of Lean lines devoted to
  passing limits is not evidence that the limiting procedure itself
  originated in 2026.

  MRS93 p.327 supplies an older fixed-IR UV limit with a different
  field/gauge scope and stated proof-detail limitations. Seiler82 pp.100\U101
  separates scaling-limit existence, independence of physical normalization
  and Euclidean invariance, warning that the independence claim need not
  hold. The Lean quantifiers at <hlink|<verbatim|PublicWilson.lean:83\U90>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/PublicWilson.lean#L83-L90>
  and <hlink|<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean:184\U188>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean#L184-L188>
  should therefore be compared individually, rather than treated as one
  generic \Pcontinuum limit.\Q

  <subsection|4.7 Rotation restoration, phases and
  nonfreeness><label|47-rotation-restoration-phases-and-nonfreeness>

  N3 ŸŸ10.2\U10.3, pp.84\U87, compares inclined blockings and generates a
  dense rotation subgroup. Lean uses local stress identities, exterior flux,
  analytic continuation and finite-dimensional label comparison.
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Ward/Walk.lean:15\U26>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Ward/Walk.lean#L15-L26>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Ward/Walk.lean:60\U84>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Ward/Walk.lean#L60-L84>
  supplies the analytic continuation along segments/walks;
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Ward/Axis.lean:86\U107>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Ward/Axis.lean#L86-L107>,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Ward/Axis.lean:130\U139>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Ward/Axis.lean#L130-L139>
  reaches collinear configurations and completes the density argument. The
  common goal of Euclidean covariance does not make these the same idea.

  N3 Ÿ11.2 obtains its unique vacuum through exponential clustering. Lean
  decomposes the zero-energy algebra into characters,
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Phase/Mixture.lean:65\U86>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Phase/Mixture.lean#L65-L86>,
  then reconstructs each phase. The character space need not contain more
  than one inequivalent phase. The same mathematical object satisfying all
  later conclusions is a standard requirement, made explicit by the final
  assembly.

  N3 Ÿ11.3, pp.93\U94, detects non-Gaussianity by a nonzero fourth spin
  cumulant. Lean's trace inverse logarithm rules out the specified finite
  mass-shell Wick profiles, <hlink|<verbatim|YM/TraceExtension/Wick/Refute.lean:75\U105>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/Wick/Refute.lean#L75-L105>.
  Those are different witnesses, and neither should be described as a
  complete classification of all conceivable generalized free models. The
  continuous-mass generalized-free case is expressly outside the exclusion at
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/TraceExtension/Wick/Refute.lean:4\U8>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/Wick/Refute.lean#L4-L8>.

  <section|5. What appears new, and what is
  routine><label|5-what-appears-new-and-what-is-routine>

  <subsection|5.1 Ranked candidates for substantive new
  work><label|51-ranked-candidates-for-substantive-new-work>

  This ranks <strong|bounded candidates for substantive constructive work>,
  not established priority. The strongest evidence concerns the precise
  estimates and their compatibility; the organizing physical principles are
  older. Generic gauge synchronization, analytic stationary branches and
  background regularity have direct older antecedents.

  <\enumerate>
    <item><strong|One renormalized gauge-composite hierarchy with
    simultaneous identifications>. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SharpGram/Assembly/Flowers.lean:75\U130>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SharpGram/Assembly/Flowers.lean#L75-L130>,
    <hlink|<verbatim|YM/MergeProof/Powder.lean:19\U71>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/MergeProof/Powder.lean#L19-L71>
    and <hlink|<verbatim|Construction.lean:15\U31>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/Construction.lean#L15-L31>
    combine finite composite labels, Maxwell quotient/rank, stress, phases
    and OPE on one family. MRS93 p.329 expressly excludes the physical
    composite-operator construction, although ŸVIII pp.377\U378 includes
    arbitrary elementary field insertions; B-Large-II pp.355\U356 leaves
    gauge observables for later work; N3 ŸŸ9\U11 has the narrower spin-field
    hierarchy and different massive endpoint. <strong|Confidence: medium> for
    this specific corpus-relative extension, low for worldwide novelty.
    Neither \Pone family\Q nor \Pall insertions\Q alone distinguishes it;
    broader constructive composite-field literature remains incompletely
    compared.

    <item><strong|Stress-driven restoration of rotations with exterior-flux
    control without an assumed gap>. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean:49\U77>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean#L49-L77>,
    <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean:105\U114>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean#L105-L114>,
    <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Route.lean:49\U67>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Route.lean#L49-L67>
    and <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Ward/Walk.lean:60\U84>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Ward/Walk.lean#L60-L84>
    give the route. N3 ŸŸ10\U11 uses inclined regulators and massive
    estimates; MRS93 p.327 leaves the full OS setting outside its fixed-IR
    endpoint; Seiler82 p.101 presents Euclidean invariance as a requirement,
    not this construction. <strong|Confidence: medium> within the corpus, low
    for priority over wider Euclidean/algebraic-QFT literature. The full
    exterior-flux argument still requires separate verification.

    <item><strong|A constructive all-radii trace two-point law and its
    specified nonfree exclusion>. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/TraceExtension/Verse/Cape.lean:53\U88>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/Verse/Cape.lean#L53-L88>,
    <hlink|<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean:168\U175>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean#L168-L175>
    and <hlink|<verbatim|YM/TraceExtension/Wick/Refute.lean:75\U105>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/TraceExtension/Wick/Refute.lean#L75-L105>
    are the precise targets. CDJ77 pp.442\U445, Eqs.(3.19)\U(3.21), gives a
    detailed older beta-function/operator-mixing trace law, including the
    distinction between the full relation and its on-shell simplification. N3
    Ÿ11.3 uses a spin cumulant instead. <strong|Confidence: medium> for the
    specific constructive realization, low for novelty of its physical
    prediction or of using a trace anomaly to detect interaction. The
    coefficient/stress matching remains a separate technical obligation.

    <item><strong|Quantitative finite-order OPE bounds on the common
    hierarchy, uniform over phases and allowed spectator positions for each
    fixed word>. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/OPE/Orbits/Kernel.lean:52\U77>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/OPE/Orbits/Kernel.lean#L52-L77>
    and <hlink|<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean:70\U80>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/ChallengeSpec.lean#L70-L80>
    give the common prescription and power-with-slack bounds. W69 ŸII
    pp.1500\U1501 Eq.(2.2) already has finite-order weak/composite expansions
    and state-independent coefficients. Z73's full text remains unavailable;
    its abstract gives a perturbative scalar comparison. MRS93 p.329 does not
    supply the physical composite construction. <strong|Confidence: medium>
    for the quantitative corpus-relative extension, low for worldwide
    novelty. Common coefficients, composites and finite-order truncation
    alone are not new; neither uniqueness nor convergence in OPE order is
    asserted.

    <item><strong|Controlled realization of the particular tagged,
    clipped/masked, compulsory-inventory gauge density transformation>.
    <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/Block.lean:39\U79>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/Block.lean#L39-L79>,
    <hlink|<verbatim|YM/RG/Activities.lean:42\U129>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/RG/Activities.lean#L42-L129>,
    <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Windows.lean:65\U74>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SectorNext/GAUSSIAN_SECTOR_CHANGE/Windows.lean#L65-L74>
    and <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/STATIONARY/Level/Transition.lean:153\U178>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/STATIONARY/Level/Transition.lean#L153-L178>
    locate the combination. N2 Ÿ5 pp.38\U55 is the closest complete formula
    comparison; GK85 p.212 Eq.(4.8) and pp.218\U221 already supplies
    compulsory covering and exact reassembly; B-Var pp.279,295\U296 and
    B-RG-I ŸŸ4\U5 supplies the gauge background/marginal programme; MRS93
    ŸŸVI\UVIII pp.368\U378 supplies another four-dimensional gauge
    stability/flow construction. <strong|Confidence: medium-low> for novelty
    of the exact combination. A different representation is not by itself a
    new estimate, and the full stationary determinant bridge has not been
    verified. The broad idea of closing a gauge RG with controlled marginal
    flow is an older antecedent.

    <item><strong|The particular finite-torus normalization estimate and its
    use with composite detectors>. <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/RANK/Determinant.lean:240\U274>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/RANK/Determinant.lean#L240-L274>
    compares exponents <math|3*V+1> and <math|3*V-3>;
    <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/RANK/Stamp.lean:131\U167>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/RANK/Stamp.lean#L131-L167>
    converts the residual global power into a cell-root cost;
    <hlink|<verbatim|YM/MergeProof/Powder.lean:48\U71>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/MergeProof/Powder.lean#L48-L71>
    separately supplies detector/rank data. MRS93 p.368 Eq.(VI.1), Lemma VI.1
    p.369 and B-Large-II pp.356\U359 are substantial normalization
    antecedents. Seiler82 pp.118\U121 concerns external-field matter
    determinants, not this pure-gauge count. N3 ŸŸ7,9 uses different
    rough-region and spin-source comparisons. <strong|Confidence: low> for
    novelty of the torus estimate, medium for the specific combined detector
    application relative to the sources read. The first exponent is ordinary
    spanning-tree counting; the exact sweep and uniform hypotheses remain
    unmatched, not proved historically new.
  </enumerate>

  No item is established as a new physical principle. Potential novelty lies
  in rigorous quantitative control and compatibility in the stated setting.
  Source-level formalization makes dependencies and quantifiers explicit;
  code length does not measure mathematical originality.

