  <section|6. Open questions, limits and priorities for expert
  checking><label|6-open-questions-limits-and-priorities-for-expert-checking>

  <subsection|6.1 Coverage and source reliability><label|61-coverage-and-source-reliability>

  The source reading is selective: it does not cover every line of Lean or
  every proof in the literature. The counts of declarations used by the main
  theorem rest on existing dependency information and source intervals, and
  do not independently establish the theorem\<#2019\>s correctness. At the
  principal citations, definitions and proof bodies support the comparisons;
  comments alone are not treated as theorems. The comparisons refer to the
  pinned Lean source and dated primary papers; they do not depend on
  revisions to secondary expositions.

  Full texts of all six OpenAI preprints and the 24 works in Ÿ7.3 were
  available. The reading covered introductions, theorem statements, overall
  structure and the technical passages cited here, with particular attention
  to N2 Ÿ5 and N3's comparison and continuum sections. This is selective
  full-text reading, not a claim that every paper or proof was read in full.
  Human rewrites and assessments are not evidence for the provenance claims,
  and the papers' own claims of originality are not treated as established
  historical facts.

  The additional sources, including the five 1985 Baªaban gauge papers,
  MRS93, GK85, BY90, Seiler82, OS73/OS75, CDJ77 and W69, were read in the
  parts specified in Ÿ7.4. Full-text availability does not mean that every
  page or proof was read. Seiler's geometric appendix was available but not
  substantively used. OSe78 and Z73 remain available only as previously
  accessed abstracts. The background references in Ÿ7.5 were not directly
  read. None of these access levels amounts to independent human endorsement
  of the comparisons.

  <subsection|6.2 Missing or incompletely accessed
  literature><label|62-missing-or-incompletely-accessed-literature>

  The five 1985 Baªaban gauge papers, MRS93, GK85, BY90, Seiler82, OS73/OS75,
  CDJ77 and W69 are available in full text. Relevant sections were read in
  part, with exact extents in Ÿ7. Availability is not a claim to have checked
  every proof. The old access-based uncertainty about whether Baªaban treated
  analytic stationary backgrounds and regular gauge transfer is resolved;
  exact theorem equivalence with the Lean implementation is not.

  <strong|Still unavailable in full:> Osterwalder\USeiler, <em|Annals of
  Physics> 110 (1978), 440\U471, and Zimmermann, <em|Annals of Physics> 77
  (1973), 570\U601. Their previously accessed abstracts remain the evidence
  limit. Seiler's exposition does not silently upgrade access to the
  Osterwalder\USeiler original.

  Reading remains incomplete for the broader Gaw¦dzki\UKupiainen corpus
  beyond GK85, Brydges\UDimock\UHurd, Feldman\UMagnen\URivasseau\USénéor,
  modern rigorous composite/OPE constructions, and older algebraic-QFT phase
  decompositions. Seiler's cited gauge-fixing and gauge-chessboard extensions
  also remain incompletely followed. The targeted determinant passages do not
  establish a predecessor or priority for the exact torus sweep.

  MRS93 pp.326\U327 explicitly presents an account short of a fully detailed
  convergence proof; B-Var p.308 omits detailed derivative calculations;
  Seiler82 p.121 explicitly labels its four-dimensional external-field
  determinant statement a Quasi-Theorem. Those qualifications are part of the
  sources' scope. They neither erase the antecedent ideas nor certify the
  Lean estimates. Polyakov, Hasenfratz, Zinn-Justin and Lüscher remain
  literature leads unless a specific result is directly matched.

  <subsection|6.3 Highest-value unresolved technical
  comparisons><label|63-highest-value-unresolved-technical-comparisons>

  <\enumerate>
    <item><strong|The stationary determinant bridge.> Follow every premise
    from the blocked joint Hessian through
    <hlink|<verbatim|pole_reduced>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/STATIONARY/Level/Transition.lean#L153-L178>
    to the auxiliary vector/ghost calculation,
    <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/STATIONARY/Level/Transition.lean:153\U178>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/STATIONARY/Level/Transition.lean#L153-L178>.
    The cited interfaces and coefficient calculation support the comparison;
    not every estimate supplying that bridge has been examined.

    <item><strong|The complete nonlinear large-field step.> Compare Balaban's
    aged-region R ratios, B-Large-I Eqs.(0.2)\U(0.6), against every actual
    Lean seed reorganization, not only the final activity interface. A
    related replacement hidden upstream would narrow the stated
    organizational difference.

    <item><strong|Gauge normalization priority.> Compare the specific torus
    <verbatim|3V+1> versus <verbatim|3V\<minus\>3> sweep in
    <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/RANK/Determinant.lean:240\U274>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/RANK/Determinant.lean#L240-L274>
    with further finite-volume gauge literature and Seiler's references.
    MRS93 ŸVI already supplies substantive background determinant stability;
    Seiler82 Ÿ6b treats a different external-field matter problem. No exact
    match was found in the targeted passages; worldwide novelty is not
    established.

    <item><strong|All-label uniformity and quantifier order.> Check that the
    intended estimates survive the growing orders in
    <hlink|<verbatim|YM/SharpGram/Assembly/Flowers.lean:75\U130>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/SharpGram/Assembly/Flowers.lean#L75-L130>
    and the pre-block order of constants. Source similarity does not settle
    that mathematical obligation.

    <item><strong|Exterior flux and phase theory.> Read all upstream spectral
    estimates used by <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean:49\U77>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Next/ROTATIONS/Yard.lean#L49-L77>,
    and compare the train-commutation phase argument with older Euclidean
    extremal-state methods.

    <item><strong|Reconstruction and contacts.> Compare the exact graded Lean
    domains and joint distributional boundary estimates with OS75 ŸIII p.285
    and ŸIV.1 p.287 Eq.(4.1). The conceptual mechanism is settled; theorem
    equivalence is not. Keep the separate contact extension within its stated
    scope.

    <item><strong|Trace normalization and physical interpretation.> Check the
    stress normalization and field/coupling conventions against CDJ77
    pp.442\U445 Eqs.(3.19)\U(3.21), including gauge/ghost/equation-of-motion
    terms and the on-shell simplification. Matching the internal running
    coordinate and renormalized composite normalization to a physical scheme
    remains unresolved.

    <item><strong|Causal provenance.> Draft histories, author testimony and
    generation records would be needed to decide which sources actually
    influenced the authors. Mathematical matching alone cannot answer that
    historical question.
  </enumerate>

  These limits do not erase the strongest positive comparisons: the matched
  retained-density formulas remain direct evidence. They do prevent upgrading
  \Pclosest inspected antecedent\Q to a proved history of invention or
  copying.

  <section|7. Bibliography and source access><label|7-bibliography-and-source-access>

  <subsection|7.1 Lean source and orientation><label|71-lean-source-and-orientation>

  The mathematical comparisons use the <hlink|released Lean source at commit
  <verbatim|3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/tree/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464>.
  The line links throughout this page identify the relevant definitions and
  proofs. The comparisons and size counts concern the source text and do not
  independently establish the theorem\<#2019\>s correctness.

  For orientation, see <hlink|statement.md|statement.tm>,
  <hlink|summary.md|summary.tm>, the <hlink|architecture|outline/index.tm>
  and the <hlink|units|outline/units.tm>. These expositions are not primary
  evidence for mathematical antecedents.

  <subsection|7.2 Six OpenAI preprints><label|72-six-openai-preprints>

  All six are preprints credited to OpenAI, not journal articles. Their full
  texts were available and read selectively for their structure and the
  mechanisms cited above. Links point to their dated folders in
  <hlink|OpenAI's math preprints|https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints>;
  the content comparisons rest on the dated texts, not on subsequent changes
  to that collection.

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item><strong|N1>: OpenAI, <em|Exponential decay in two-dimensional
    classical O(n) models>, September 23, 2026, 20 printed pages.
    <hlink|Preprint|https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/Exponential-decay-in-two-dimensional-classical-On-models-September-23-2026>.

    <item><strong|N2>: OpenAI, <em|Sharp mass bounds for the two-dimensional
    O(4) model>, September 23, 2026, 102 printed pages.
    <hlink|Preprint|https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/Sharp-mass-bounds-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-model-September-23-2026>.

    <item><strong|N3>: OpenAI, <em|The canonical massive continuum limit of
    the two-dimensional O(3) model>, October 4, 2026, 122 printed pages.
    <hlink|Preprint|https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/The-canonical-massive-continuum-limit-of-the-two-dimensional-O3-model-October-4-2026>.

    <item><strong|N4>: OpenAI, <em|An Isolated Particle Pole for the
    Two-Dimensional O(3) Spin Field>, October 4, 2026, 26 printed pages.
    <hlink|Preprint|https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/An-Isolated-Particle-Pole-for-the-Two-Dimensional-O3-Spin-Field-October-4-2026>.

    <item><strong|N5>: OpenAI, <em|Exact mass asymptotics for the
    two-dimensional O(4) lattice model>, October 5, 2026, 109 printed pages.
    <hlink|Preprint|https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/Exact-mass-asymptotics-for-the-two-dimensional-O4-lattice-model-October-5-2026>.

    <item><strong|N6>: OpenAI, <em|The Critical Spin Field of the Planar XY
    Model>, October 5, 2026, 43 printed pages.
    <hlink|Preprint|https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/The-Critical-Spin-Field-of-the-Planar-XY-Model-October-5-2026>.
  </itemize-minus>

  <subsection|7.3 Balaban and related works: 24
  sources><label|73-balaban-and-related-works-24-sources>

  Full texts of these 24 works were available; introductions, theorem
  statements and selected relevant technical sections were read. This is not
  a claim of complete proof-by-proof reading. The descriptions identify each
  work's subject so that gauge, spin and scalar results remain distinct.

  <\enumerate>
    <item><strong|B-Green.> Tadeusz Baªaban, \PRegularity and Decay of
    Lattice Green's Functions,\Q <em|Communications in Mathematical Physics>
    <strong|89> (1983), 571\U597. Gauge-covariant difference operators; Ÿ2
    generalized random walks.

    <item><strong|B-Prop-I.> T. Baªaban, \PPropagators and Renormalization
    Transformations for Lattice Gauge Theories. I,\Q <em|Communications in
    Mathematical Physics> <strong|95> (1984), 17\U40. Gaussian vector gauge
    theory, block averages and gauge change.

    <item><strong|B-Prop-II.> T. Baªaban, \PPropagators and Renormalization
    Transformations for Lattice Gauge Theories. II,\Q <em|Communications in
    Mathematical Physics> <strong|96> (1984), 223\U250. Restrictions on
    several scales and irregular domains.

    <item><strong|B-RG-I.> Tadeusz Baªaban, \PRenormalization Group Approach
    to Lattice Gauge Field Theories. I. Generation of Effective Actions in a
    Small Field Approximation and a Coupling Constant Renormalization in Four
    Dimensions,\Q <em|Communications in Mathematical Physics> <strong|109>
    (1987), 249\U301. Small-field effective actions, Ward identities,
    marginal extraction.

    <item><strong|B-RG-II.> Tadeusz Baªaban, \PRenormalization Group Approach
    to Lattice Gauge Field Theories. II. Cluster Expansions,\Q
    <em|Communications in Mathematical Physics> <strong|116> (1988), 1\U22.
    Localization and exponentiated connected expansion.

    <item><strong|B-Conv.> T. Baªaban, \PConvergent Renormalization
    Expansions for Lattice Gauge Theories,\Q <em|Communications in
    Mathematical Physics> <strong|119> (1988), 243\U285. Full density with
    large-field domains; superrenormalizable closure.

    <item><strong|B-Large-I.> Tadeusz Baªaban, \PLarge Field Renormalization.
    I. The Basic Step of the R Operation,\Q <em|Communications in
    Mathematical Physics> <strong|122> (1989), 175\U202. Repeated
    renormalization of large-field densities.

    <item><strong|B-Large-II.> Tadeusz Baªaban, \PLarge Field
    Renormalization. II. Localization, Exponentiation, and Bounds for the R
    Operation,\Q <em|Communications in Mathematical Physics> <strong|122>
    (1989), 355\U392. Conditional four-dimensional ultraviolet stability.

    <item><strong|B-Higgs.> Tadeusz Baªaban, \P(Higgs)\<#2082\>,\<#2083\>
    Quantum Fields in a Finite Volume. I. A Lower Bound,\Q <em|Communications
    in Mathematical Physics> <strong|85> (1982), <strong|603\U626>.
    Scalar/vector model in dimensions two and three, not pure
    four-dimensional YM. The original first-page header itself says 603\U636;
    the 24-page PDF ends at 626. The <hlink|publisher
    record|https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF01403506> confirms
    603\U626.

    <item><strong|B-N-I.> Tadeusz Baªaban, \PA Low Temperature Expansion for
    Classical N-Vector Models. I. A Renormalization Group Flow,\Q
    <em|Communications in Mathematical Physics> <strong|167> (1995),
    103\U154. Low-temperature spin-wave programme in <math|d\<geqslant\>3>.

    <item><strong|B-N-Var.> Tadeusz Baªaban, \PThe Variational Problems for
    Classical N-Vector Models,\Q <em|Communications in Mathematical Physics>
    <strong|175> (1996), 607\U642. Background minimizers, analyticity and
    regularity.

    <item><strong|B-N-Loc-I.> Tadeusz Baªaban, \PLocalization Expansions. I.
    Functions of the \<#2018\>Background\<#2019\> Configurations,\Q
    <em|Communications in Mathematical Physics> <strong|182> (1996), 33\U82.
    Localization of nonlocal minimizers and covariance functions.

    <item><strong|B-N-II.> Tadeusz Baªaban, \PA Low Temperature Expansion for
    Classical N-Vector Models. II. Renormalization Group Equations,\Q
    <em|Communications in Mathematical Physics> <strong|182> (1996),
    675\U721. N-vector RG equations, not a general gauge survey.

    <item><strong|B-N-III.> Tadeusz Baªaban, \PA Low Temperature Expansion
    for Classical N-Vector Models III. A Complete Inductive Description,
    Fluctuation Integrals,\Q <em|Communications in Mathematical Physics>
    <strong|196> (1998), 485\U521.

    <item><strong|B-N-Loc-II.> Tadeusz Baªaban, \PRenormalization and
    Localization Expansions. II. Expectation Values of the
    \<#2018\>Fluctuation\<#2019\> Measures,\Q <em|Communications in
    Mathematical Physics> <strong|198> (1998), 1\U45.

    <item><strong|B-N-Large.> Tadeusz Baªaban, \PThe Large Field
    Renormalization Operation for Classical N-Vector Models,\Q
    <em|Communications in Mathematical Physics> <strong|198> (1998),
    493\U534.

    <item><strong|B-N-Corr.> Tadeusz Baªaban and Michael O'Carroll, \PLow
    Temperature Properties for Correlation Functions in Classical N-Vector
    Spin Models,\Q <em|Communications in Mathematical Physics> <strong|199>
    (1999), 493\U520. Magnetization and Goldstone asymptotics in
    <math|d\<geqslant\>3>.

    <item><strong|B-Corr.> Tadeusz Baªaban and Michael O'Carroll, \PA Simple
    Method for Correlation Functions via the Effective Actions in the
    Renormalization Group Framework,\Q <em|Annals of Physics> <strong|260>
    (1997), 1\U8.

    The worked model is the massive two-dimensional Gross\UNeveu model, not
    NLSM or YM.

    <item><strong|B-Spin.> Tadeusz Baªaban, \PA Low Temperature Expansion and
    \<#2018\>Spin Wave Picture\<#2019\> for Classical N-Vector Models,\Q in
    V. Rivasseau (ed.), <em|Constructive Physics: Results in Field Theory,
    Statistical Mechanics and Condensed Matter Physics>, Lecture Notes in
    Physics <strong|446> (Springer, 1995), 201\U218.

    <hlink|Publisher metadata|https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/3-540-59190-7_30>
    distinguishes the 1995 volume from 2005 online posting.

    <item><strong|D-I.> J. Dimock, \PThe Renormalization Group According to
    Balaban. I. Small Fields,\Q <em|Reviews in Mathematical Physics>
    <strong|25> (2013), 1330010; <hlink|arXiv:1108.1335|https://arxiv.org/abs/1108.1335>.
    Cited preprint version: October 28, 2018, 52 pages.

    <item><strong|D-II.> J. Dimock, \PThe Renormalization Group According to
    Balaban. II. Large Fields,\Q <em|Journal of Mathematical Physics>
    <strong|54> (2013), 092301; <hlink|arXiv:1212.5562|https://arxiv.org/abs/1212.5562>.
    Cited preprint version: August 25, 2018, 94 pages.

    <item><strong|D-III.> J. Dimock, \PThe Renormalization Group According to
    Balaban. III. Convergence,\Q <em|Annales Henri Poincaré> <strong|15>
    (2014), 2133\U2175; <hlink|arXiv:1304.0705|https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.0705>.
    Cited preprint version: March 29, 2018, 37 pages. <hlink|Publisher
    record|https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00023-013-0303-3> gives
    issue 2014, online 2013. All three Dimock papers treat scalar
    <math|\<varphi\><rsup|4>> in dimension three; they do not supply
    non-Abelian gauge arguments.

    <item><strong|Bry.> David C. Brydges, \PA Short Course on Cluster
    Expansions,\Q in K. Osterwalder and R. Stora (eds.), <em|Critical
    Phenomena, Random Systems, Gauge Theories>, Les Houches Session XLIII
    (1984), Part I, North-Holland/Elsevier (1986), 129\U183. Published in
    1986 from the 1984 lectures.

    <item><strong|Cam.> Camillo Cammarota, \PDecay of Correlations for
    Infinite Range Interactions in Unbounded Spin Systems,\Q
    <em|Communications in Mathematical Physics> <strong|85> (1982), 517\U528.
    Polymer convergence and decay; not authored by Baªaban.
  </enumerate>

